var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-_,:!=\\[\\]()\\\\\"`/]+|\\.(?!\\d)"},"items":[{"location":"index.html","level":1,"title":"Computing resources","text":"For research and teaching. Find available services, understand the growing gap, and explore practical opportunities for the college. Find a resource →Develop a resource request →","path":["Overview"],"tags":[]},{"location":"index.html#resources-we-can-build-on","level":2,"title":"Resources we can build on","text":"<p>Start with college and campus compute, software, data services and technical expertise. The resource inventory brings access, costs, contacts and limitations together. Match a service to the activity and confirm capacity with its owner.</p>","path":["Overview"],"tags":[]},{"location":"index.html#the-growing-gap","level":2,"title":"The growing gap","text":"What we have previously relied on can no longer be guaranteed <p>Demand spans compute, software, data and the people who make them usable. Meanwhile, some free services have closed, shortened access or tightened limits. Useful options remain, but a previous semester’s setup is no longer a sufficient continuity plan. The report distinguishes documented changes from unknowns; it does not measure a college-wide shortfall.</p>","path":["Overview"],"tags":[]},{"location":"index.html#the-opportunity","level":2,"title":"The opportunity","text":"01 / CONNECTMake existing resources usable<p>Clarify access, onboarding and support before duplicating services.</p>02 / SCOPEDescribe the work and the gap<p>Capture the activity, barriers and support needs. Technical quantities can remain unknown.</p>03 / TESTBuild evidence for shared routes<p>Compare reusable environments, shared expertise, partnerships and allocation-backed services.</p> <p>For the instructional pilot, a measured ACCESS CPU + GPU allocation is the proposed route; eligible NAIRR Classroom pilots may complement it. Awards, operations and classroom access still need confirmation. The group can compare this with other shared-service approaches.</p> Read the working-group primers →Read the report →Browse supplements →","path":["Overview"],"tags":[]},{"location":"index.html#working-group-context","level":2,"title":"Working-group context","text":"<p>End of Fall 2026: preliminary inventory, needs assessment, approaches and recommendations. Early Spring 2027: refinement and intra-college circulation. Working materials →</p> This is a working resource. College needs, available support hours, funding and service commitments still require confirmation.","path":["Overview"],"tags":[]},{"location":"access-request.html","level":1,"title":"ACCESS CPU + GPU request","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Draft only; not submitted or awarded. ACCESS is the intended large classroom CPU + GPU allocation. Campus AWS is only for CPU component tests. Free GPUs support benchmarking before the pilot.</p>","path":["ACCESS CPU + GPU request"],"tags":[]},{"location":"access-request.html#complete-before-submission","level":2,"title":"Complete before submission","text":"<ul> <li>Eligible PI, actual course leads, term, enrollment and resource match.</li> <li>Measured CPU/GPU components, concurrency, memory, storage and software needs.</li> <li>Provider exchange/billing units, access window, class/project account structure and permitted data.</li> <li>Named operators, independent backup, support commitments and any confirmed eligible grant needs.</li> </ul> <p>Download editable ACCESS request text · Existing ACCESS draft PDF</p>","path":["ACCESS CPU + GPU request"],"tags":[]},{"location":"access-request.html#conditional-alternatives","level":2,"title":"Conditional alternatives","text":"<p>CloudBank: ask whether an eligible ACCESS/CloudBank route can provide the managed service required. Do not assume approval, credits or an award. Optional concept text.</p> <p>NAIRR Classroom: pursue eligible instructor AI pilots in parallel where useful. Verify course/instructor criteria and reporting; this is not an assumed award or a substitute for the whole baseline.</p> <p>Needs tool · Readiness checks · Full request context</p>","path":["ACCESS CPU + GPU request"],"tags":[]},{"location":"approaches.html","level":1,"title":"Portfolio of approaches","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Candidates for investigation, not selected investments. Combine approaches when useful; compare their support and recurring obligations as carefully as their technical benefits.</p> Approach Opportunity First useful test Main dependency Improve use of campus resources Reduce duplicate purchases and onboarding friction Map one research and one teaching workflow to documented campus routes Eligibility, headroom, support and class-use terms Shared RSE and technical support Pool scarce expertise and reusable environments Estimate a few tasks and trial a bounded support window Named staff, existing commitments and funding; no new appointment assumed Shared data and software stewardship Reuse datasets, metadata and environments safely Review a dataset/license and test a portable workflow Rights, permissions, accessibility and renewal ownership Allocation-backed compute service Support larger CPU/GPU work without relying only on free tiers Benchmark components and validate a target route An award, operations capacity and provider terms; ACCESS pilot remains conditional Efficient models and workflows Lower unnecessary compute and improve reproducibility Compare a smaller model, retrieval approach or checkpointed job against a quality baseline Task-specific evaluation; savings must be measured Partnerships and shared infrastructure Connect compatible college, campus and external efforts Define a small joint use case with an interested partner Agreed responsibilities, access and sustainable support Reusable teaching and evaluation tools Avoid rebuilding assignment environments and grading workflows Test exports and a synthetic class before adoption Maintainer continuity, account terms and separate compute quotas","path":["Portfolio of approaches"],"tags":[]},{"location":"approaches.html#use-one-comparison-standard","level":2,"title":"Use one comparison standard","text":"<p>For each candidate, document instructional/research benefit, users served, accessibility, data suitability, required effort, recurring costs, funding status, ownership and a reversible test. Do not rank options using invented cost savings or unmeasured capacity.</p> <p>Draft recommendations · Resource inventory · Supporting compute pilot</p>","path":["Portfolio of approaches"],"tags":[]},{"location":"approaches.html#drafting-support","level":2,"title":"Drafting support","text":"<p>The resource tool prepares a local brief and sourced request template. Optional Luna drafting needs explicit consent for public/synthetic material. Technical assessment, limits and model trials are supplementary.</p>","path":["Portfolio of approaches"],"tags":[]},{"location":"budget.html","level":1,"title":"Time &amp; funding","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Greg: 10 hours/week total. Cash budget: $0. Those hours cover all duties, including EduCloud. The functions below divide one envelope; they are not separate staff positions.</p> Function Potential hours over 23 weeks Allocation and coordination 22.0 Benchmark and component work 34.0 Service operations 78 Instructional design and accessibility 14.5 Data, rights and recovery review 25 EduCloud grant preparation 23 Planning and incident buffer 33.5 <ul> <li>Two course support TAs: 102 potential hours over the modeled period; separate from Greg.</li> <li>Two course leads: 70 potential hours over the modeled period; separate from Greg.</li> </ul> <p>Course leads need no salary. No college sponsor or finance role is included. Regular teaching/grading is outside these added pilot hours. No appointment, funding or backup commitment is established by this plan.</p>","path":["Time &amp; funding"],"tags":[]},{"location":"budget.html#grant-dependencies","level":2,"title":"Grant dependencies","text":"<p>Any paid need must be covered by confirmed eligible grant funding under the EduCloud plan. Separate development, training, evaluation and recurring operations; avoid double counting hours or assuming that an application is an award. An allocation supplies resources, not automatically staffing or durable managed hosting.</p> <p>Detailed hours CSV · Grant work packages · Scope support demand</p> <p>If the hours do not fit: reduce the pilot scope or secure actual additional support before class. The independent backup remains unfilled.</p>","path":["Time &amp; funding"],"tags":[]},{"location":"campus-ai.html","level":1,"title":"Arizona AI Assistant transition","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>AI Verde → Arizona AI Assistant. Wolfgang Jetner at the Office of Responsible AI (ORAI) is the source of this planned transition and $200/month of AWS Bedrock credit, relayed by Greg Chism. Transition date, who receives the credit, eligibility, renewal and overage controls need confirmation. This is an October 19, 2026 working-group update, not a verified entitlement for every faculty member or student.</p> <p>Public university news describes Arizona AI Assistant as a fall pilot and AI Verde separately. The new portal returned HTTP 200; we have not tested a signed-in account or campus API. Contact the Office of Responsible AI for current enrollment and credit terms; Mithun Paul (mithunpaul@arizona.edu) is the previously published AI Verde contact for migration questions.</p>","path":["Arizona AI Assistant transition"],"tags":[]},{"location":"campus-ai.html#models-and-prices-in-the-supplied-screenshot","level":2,"title":"Models and prices in the supplied screenshot","text":"<p>USD per one million tokens. These are campus gateway prices in the ORAI screenshot from Wolfgang Jetner, relayed by Greg Chism, not direct OpenAI prices or measured bills. Check current account rates before use.</p> Model ID Input Output <code>gpt-5.6-luna</code> $0.22 $1.32 <code>gpt-5.6-sol</code> $4.40 $22.00 <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> $2.20 $11.00 <code>claude-haiku-4-5-20251001</code> $1.10 $5.50 <code>claude-opus-5</code> $5.50 $27.50 <code>claude-opus-5-5</code> $4.40 $22.00 <code>claude-sonnet-5</code> $2.20 $11.00 <code>gpt-5.6-terra</code> $2.20 $13.20 <code>gpt-6-luna</code> $0.11 $0.55 <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code> $0.11 $0.55 <code>claude-sonnet-5-5</code> $2.20 $11.00 <code>google.gemma-4-31b</code> $0.14 $0.40 <p>View supplied pricing screenshot · Received October 19, 2026; capture and effective dates are unconfirmed.</p> <p>For example, at the listed campus Luna rate, 10,000 input + 1,000 output tokens = $0.00165 before any other charges. This is illustrative arithmetic, not an observed bill or promised credit balance. Credit provides model inference, not a reserved GPU, training cluster or guaranteed class concurrency.</p>","path":["Arizona AI Assistant transition"],"tags":[]},{"location":"campus-ai.html#before-relying-on-the-replacement","level":2,"title":"Before relying on the replacement","text":"<ul> <li>Confirm migration timing, existing team/workspace exports, API compatibility and the correct API base URL.</li> <li>Confirm whether $200/month is per person, team or another allocation; determine reset, expiry and overage behavior.</li> <li>Review allowed data, retention, model availability and throughput under campus terms.</li> <li>Test one public/synthetic workflow and a classroom rehearsal before required use.</li> </ul> <p>The resource request tool currently uses the separate direct OpenAI Luna pilot. Its $1 monthly application allowance does not draw from campus Bedrock credit. Campus integration needs a campus-issued credential and confirmed API terms.</p> <p>Resource inventory · Drafting assessment</p>","path":["Arizona AI Assistant transition"],"tags":[]},{"location":"classroom-assessment.html","level":1,"title":"Route assessment","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Choose the service model before choosing a machine.</p> Work Route to assess Verify first Required interactive CPU + GPU teaching ACCESS resource/provider; conditional managed cloud option Accounts, scheduling, simultaneous sessions, persistent homes, support and full term Short independent research batches OSPool; eligible PATh Facility or ACCESS Interruptions, checkpointing, data policy and project eligibility Instructor-led AI pilot NAIRR Classroom; eligible notebook/model services Instructor eligibility, course scope, quota, reporting and permitted data Bounded experiments Codespaces, Kaggle, Colab, conditional credits Limits, queues, card requirements, output export and fallback Durable apps, databases and archives Separate hosting/storage/preservation service Funding, uptime, backups, ownership, retention and restore <p>Proposed pilot: two 40-student courses, a ten-person canary and a whole-class rehearsal. Every number is provisional until course leads and measurements confirm it.</p> <p>Scope needs · Federal map · Admission checks · Full assessment evidence</p>","path":["Route assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"classroom-resources.html","level":1,"title":"Classroom platforms","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Compare bounded options for development, notebooks, scoring and demonstrations. Open each resource’s limits before choosing it. GPU access is variable; competition hosting is not a GPU notebook.</p> <p>Benchmark first · Options by degree · Cloud credits</p> Search resourcesResource typeAll typesclouddemodevelopevaluatelegacymanage<p>22 resources</p>manageClassroom 50<p>Workable successor for GitHub Classroom assignment distribution, repositories, submissions, feedback and grading.</p><p>Access: Free, open-source assignment workflow · evaluate before adoption</p>Limits and sources<p>Fifty Foundation project and hosted app are not ours. Requires a Team/Enterprise organization; check education eligibility. No additional Codespaces compute; Actions grading has separate quotas. ACCESS remains the CPU/GPU classroom route.</p><p>Classroom 50: project and license · Classroom 50: prerequisites and education eligibility · Classroom 50: managing Actions cost · Codespaces · free personal-account quotas</p>evaluateCodabench<p>Prediction scoring, reproducible code benchmarks and common test environments.</p><p>Access: Free benchmark hosting; compute depends on queue</p>Limits and sources<p>Shared CPU scoring jobs ≤20 minutes. Organizers supply queues for code submissions and all GPU workers; no GPU notebook or whole-class reservation.</p><p>Codabench · free benchmark hosting and bounded CPU scoring · Codabench · organizer-supplied CPU / GPU workers · Codabench · shared and dedicated queues</p>evaluateKaggle Community Competitions<p>Prediction-file competitions with live leaderboards; judged hackathons for open-ended projects.</p><p>Access: Free self-service hosts and participants</p>Limits and sources<p>Training happens elsewhere or in separately limited notebooks. Restricted-email competitions disable notebooks by default; community hosting has no hands-on support.</p><p>Kaggle · no-cost Community Competitions · Kaggle · competition access and notebook limits</p>evaluateCodeGrade<p>Programming labs with browser editing, immediate autograding and instructor feedback.</p><p>Access: Advertised free for courses ≤50 students</p>Limits and sources<p>Check free-plan execution/LMS limits and roster scope. No verified free GPU entitlement or campus deployment; grading records need approved retention.</p><p>CodeGrade · bounded free classroom autograding</p>evaluateChaGrade<p>Challenge-linked individual/team homework and instructor grading beyond leaderboard scores.</p><p>Access: Adjacent project; current free entitlement unverified</p>Limits and sources<p>Live page labels version 0.1 / Work In Progress. Support, quotas and current Codabench integration need verification; not a ready college-wide baseline.</p><p>ChaGrade · challenge-linked classroom workflow</p>evaluateEvalAI<p>Custom prediction/code evaluation and organizer-controlled remote workers.</p><p>Access: Hosted service paid; open-source self-hosting</p>Limits and sources<p>Hosted plans $125–850/month; remote evaluation $100/month plus own compute. Self-hosting requires funded staff, infrastructure and storage. No verified hosted free tier.</p><p>EvalAI · paid hosted challenge plans · EvalAI · open-source self-hosting</p>legacyCodaLab Competitions<p>Historical challenge service; migrate compatible competition bundles to Codabench.</p><p>Access: Retired at end of 2025; successor available</p>Limits and sources<p>New account required; leaderboards do not transfer automatically. Preserve submissions/results separately. Separate CodaLab Worksheets is not covered.</p><p>CodaLab Competitions · phased out in 2025 · Codabench · CodaLab migration boundaries</p>developGitHub Codespaces<p>Reproducible Python/R/SQL labs, small analysis and code development before submission.</p><p>Access: Bounded free personal CPU quotas</p>Limits and sources<p>60 two-core hours/month, or 90 for verified students; storage quota and machine sizes matter. No generally available GPU; not continuous application hosting.</p><p>Codespaces · free personal-account quotas · Codespaces · verified student benefit · Codespaces · machine learning and GPU boundary</p>developKaggle Notebooks<p>Checkpointed small-model GPU experiments and training for a competition.</p><p>Access: Free accelerator notebooks; quota varies</p>Limits and sources<p>Check each account’s GPU quota, available hardware, session timer and saved disk. No reserved class seats or distributed/long-running training promise.</p><p>Kaggle · free accelerator notebooks · Kaggle · floating weekly GPU quota</p>developFree Google Colab<p>Short shared notebooks, CPU analysis and opportunistic accelerator exercises.</p><p>Access: Base free tier remains available</p>Limits and sources<p>GPU access and resource/session limits fluctuate. Free Colab is separate from the closed new-signup education Pro offer; never promise class GPU availability.</p><p>Google Colab Pro for Education · offer closed</p>developmybinder.org<p>Small Python/R notebooks launched from a reproducible repository, including metadata/text workshops.</p><p>Access: Free ephemeral CPU sessions</p>Limits and sources<p>1–2 GB RAM; ten-minute idle shutdown; aims for ≤6 hours or one CPU-hour; 100 concurrent sessions per repo. Download work; no GPU or persistent workspace promise.</p><p>Binder · free ephemeral teaching notebooks</p>developLightning AI Studio<p>DS/MLIS notebook preparation and small tests within actual credit balance.</p><p>Access: One free CPU Studio; GPU credit amount and expiry vary</p>Limits and sources<p>Four-hour CPU restart boundary; first 10 GB storage free. Do not promise a fixed monthly GPU grant or count paid academic discounts as free compute.</p><p>Lightning AI · billing and storage scope · Lightning AI student documentation and free CPU workflow</p>demoHugging Face ZeroGPU / Spaces<p>Short Gradio inference demos and model-behavior comparisons; static exhibits on free Static Spaces.</p><p>Access: Five GPU minutes/day for signed-in Free users</p>Limits and sources<p>Two own ZeroGPU Spaces only for eligible personal accounts ≥30 days old. Shared queues and Gradio constraints; organization hosting paid. Ordinary compute-backed Space creation also needs a paid plan.</p><p>Hugging Face ZeroGPU · short shared GPU demos · Hugging Face Spaces · current creation and persistence boundaries</p>democuda.live<p>Tiny CUDA kernel/compiler demonstrations independent of the Codabench workflow.</p><p>Access: 15 executions/month; bounded CUDA exercises</p>Limits and sources<p>Advertised T4 16 GB, five runs per 15 minutes; per-execution allowance is not GPU-hours. Existing October 8 verification, no reserved class capacity.</p><p>cuda.live · optional CUDA micro-exercises</p>cloudModal<p>DS: a small pinned training/inference run. MLIS: public-corpus OCR, embeddings or retrieval experiments.</p><p>Access: Conditional recurring credit · $30/workspace/month; three Starter seats</p>Limits and sources<p>Payment method required; GPU, CPU and memory share credits. Set explicit usage and net-spend limits. Shared Endpoints tokens cost extra. Academic grant is an application, not funding in hand.</p><p>Modal Starter pricing and Shared Endpoints boundary · Modal billing: payment method required · Modal budgets and out-of-pocket spend limits · Modal academic credit grants</p>cloudBeam<p>DS/MLIS component hosting if funded; open-source runtime may warrant later engineering assessment.</p><p>Access: Paid / historical free claim · $20/month Developer includes $20 credits; one seat</p>Limits and sources<p>June free-$30 comparison is stale against current direct plan table. Prepaid deployments need credits; warm/idle settings can bill. BYOC is not free compute.</p><p>Beam current plan table · Beam runtime and prepaid credit billing · Beam earlier free Developer comparison (historical)</p>cloudCerebrium<p>DS model serving; MLIS public-corpus embeddings or transcription.</p><p>Access: Paid usage / possible trial · Free Hobby subscription; no recurring compute credit verified</p>Limits and sources<p>Free plan fee and included storage do not make execution free. Trials are discretionary/time-limited and may transition to paid billing.</p><p>Cerebrium Hobby subscription versus metered compute · Cerebrium trial terms</p>developPaperspace Gradient<p>Small DS training and public/synthetic MLIS corpus notebooks.</p><p>Access: Bounded free machines · Free CPU / M4000 GPU subject to availability; 5 GB</p>Limits and sources<p>Six-hour free-session maximum, one-hour idle stop, one free notebook at once; free notebooks are public. Check old GPU/runtime fit and actual signup/payment requirements.</p><p>DigitalOcean Paperspace: free machine and storage eligibility · Paperspace notebook free-session duration · Paperspace free machines: visibility and inactivity</p>developSaturn Cloud<p>Potential DS and MLIS notebook alternative, pending an authorized account check.</p><p>Access: Unconfirmed free lead · Landing page advertises 150 compute hours/month, no card</p>Limits and sources<p>150 compute hours is not verified as 150 GPU-hours. Current pricing emphasizes paid hosted resources; reconcile device allowance, storage, inactivity and new-account access.</p><p>Saturn Cloud free-compute landing claim · Saturn Cloud current hosted pricing</p>cloudRunpod<p>Funded DS/MLIS jobs with explicit checkpoint and external export.</p><p>Access: Paid comparison · Prepaid compute; no recurring free allocation verified</p>Limits and sources<p>Pod launch needs credit; stopped storage still bills and can be deleted when funds run out. Not a zero-cash fallback or preservation repository.</p><p>Runpod credit balance and storage lifecycle</p>cloudReplicate<p>DS model-behavior comparison; MLIS controlled metadata/transcription demonstrations.</p><p>Access: Limited model trial · Select-model demonstrations, then billing</p>Limits and sources<p>No stable free monthly grant verified. Private deployments can bill setup and idle time. A model API does not replace arbitrary CUDA training.</p><p>Replicate limited free models and billing scope</p>cloudHugging Face Inference Providers<p>DS inference evaluation and MLIS embeddings/metadata tasks when funded.</p><p>Access: Paid API on Free accounts · No included monthly API credits for Free users</p>Limits and sources<p>Separate from ZeroGPU/Spaces. Current API pricing overrides older free-credit snippets; no historical change date established.</p><p>Hugging Face Inference Providers: current free-account credits</p>&lt;p&gt;Search needs JavaScript; all resources are shown.&lt;/p&gt;","path":["Classroom platforms"],"tags":[]},{"location":"classroom50.html","level":1,"title":"Classroom 50","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Workable successor for GitHub Classroom’s assignment workflow; evaluate it before adoption.</p> What it can help with What it does not solve Assignment distribution, course repositories, submissions, feedback and grading workflows Reserved compute, Codespaces limits, institutional service ownership or an automatic ACCESS integration","path":["Classroom 50"],"tags":[]},{"location":"classroom50.html#ownership-and-limits","level":2,"title":"Ownership and limits","text":"<ul> <li>The Fifty Foundation maintains Classroom 50 and its hosted app. We do not own or operate that service.</li> <li>A college-controlled GitHub organization can own course repositories and configuration. That is different from owning the application service.</li> <li>Check the required Team/Enterprise organization and its education eligibility.</li> <li>Codespaces’ limited free compute allocation is unchanged. Actions-based grading has separate quotas/billing.</li> <li>GPL-3.0 self-hosting is possible in principle; no local deployment or funded operator is established here.</li> </ul>","path":["Classroom 50"],"tags":[]},{"location":"classroom50.html#before-adoption","level":2,"title":"Before adoption","text":"<p>Test with a synthetic course: app permissions, roster handling, assignment creation, feedback, Actions quotas, export and access after the term. Keep authoritative grades in the approved LMS and a portable assignment/export path.</p> <p>Classroom 50: project and license · Classroom 50: prerequisites and education eligibility · Classroom 50: managing Actions cost · Codespaces · free personal-account quotas</p>","path":["Classroom 50"],"tags":[]},{"location":"compute-estimator.html","level":1,"title":"Optional compute estimate","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Start with the short needs brief. Use this optional calculator only when you can estimate the workload; unknown technical quantities belong in the brief, not as invented numbers. Numbers are editable examples, not measured demand, provider credits or an award. No data is uploaded or saved by this tool. Enter counts and requirements only, never private records.</p> Load 40-student exampleLoad research example <p>40-student example loaded; edit the assumptions below.</p> <p></p> 1. Purpose and permissionsPurposeClassroomResearchWork patternInteractive notebooks / sessionsIndependent batch jobsContinuous service / APIData classPublic / synthetic, rights-clearedInstitutional / student recordsSensitive / regulated / restrictedInput basisUnmeasured assumptionsComponent measurements availableTarget-resource replay available<small>A selection is self-reported, not verified evidence.</small>2. Workload per runPeople or independent groups<small>Each gets the per-week runs below.</small>Active weeks<small>Course or research working period.</small>Runs per person / week<small>Use the busiest representative workload.</small>CPU-only wall hours / run<small>Exclude GPU-only work; include interactive idle time if allocated.</small>CPU cores / run<small>Configured/allocated, not assumed full utilization.</small>RAM GiB / run<small>Include the whole process/container.</small>GPU wall hours / run<small>Set 0 for CPU-only work.</small>GPU devices / GPU run<small>A visible/shared device is not automatically a full physical GPU.</small>VRAM GiB / device<small>A fit requirement, not GPU-hour conversion.</small>Simultaneous CPU jobs/sessions<small>Up to the participant/group count.</small>Simultaneous GPU jobs<small>Cannot exceed the overall peak.</small>Retry / variation allowance %<small>Applied to runtime totals only; 0–100.</small>3. Storage and support · editable defaults3. Storage and supportShared input data GiB<small>One shared copy; avoid per-person duplication here.</small>Working files GiB / person<small>Home/workspace allowance.</small>Total output data GiB<small>Across this whole scenario.</small>Independent backup copies<small>Beyond active data; 0 requires a recovery decision.</small>Retention weeks<small>How long files need to remain available.</small>Potential technical-support hours / week<small>Scope-specific estimate; no college staffing capacity is assumed.</small>Separate TA hours / week<small>Potential support; not a staffing commitment.</small>Update scenario <p></p> <p></p>Your planning envelopeCPU core-hoursGPU device-hoursGiB active + independent copies<p></p><p></p><p></p>Route to assess<p></p>Checks still requiredDownload briefDownload JSONPrint results &lt;p class=\"guide-note\"&gt;Enable JavaScript to calculate and export, or use these formulas: runs = people × weeks × runs/week; CPU core-hours = runs × CPU wall hours × cores × (1 + allowance/100); GPU device-hours = runs × GPU wall hours × devices × the same allowance. Peak demand is separate. Storage = shared inputs + people × working files + outputs; independent copies multiply that storage.&lt;/p&gt;","path":["Optional compute estimate"],"tags":[]},{"location":"compute-estimator.html#how-to-use-the-estimate","level":2,"title":"How to use the estimate","text":"<p>This is one representative workload envelope. For different CPU/GPU groups or multiple courses, export separate scenarios and review overlap before adding totals. Continuous services need uptime-based sizing beyond this per-run model. Storage capacity does not establish throughput, preservation or a backup service.</p> <p>The tool follows the benchmark helper’s runtime × workload × resource × allowance arithmetic. Use measured component results and <code>size_access.py</code> for the next sizing step, then confirm provider units, target replay and classroom checks. Neither the tool nor a selected evidence basis makes a request submission-ready.</p>","path":["Optional compute estimate"],"tags":[]},{"location":"degree-options.html","level":1,"title":"Options by degree","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>This earlier nine-degree comparison excludes cyber programs. The working group’s needs assessment covers the college and is not limited to this historical comparison. These are activity fits, not confirmed course adoptions. Free resources suit small, bounded work; larger workloads need a measured and approved route.</p> Degree Suitable small-work starting point When to move / change activity BS Information Science Python / R / SQL labs, small-data models and short GPU experiments. Browser prototypes for the immersive emphasis. Large training, concurrent GPUs, high-memory data or immersive hardware are required. ACCESS CPU / GPU labs; NAIRR Classroom for AI courses; campus XR hardware. MS Data Science Reproducible notebooks, tabular ML, sampled datasets and checkpointed deep-learning labs. Data exceeds RAM / storage, models exceed available VRAM, or jobs need long / distributed training. ACCESS for larger data and scheduled capacity; NAIRR Classroom for AI instruction. MS Information Science ML subplan: notebook experiments. Human-Centered Computing: web prototypes and small public-data studies. Training or deployment must persist; studies need controlled data, full desktops or specialized devices. Managed ACCESS environments; NAIRR Classroom where the course teaches AI. BA Emerging Technologies in Society Small public-text / network analyses, web development, digital storytelling and public portfolios. Live app backends, licensed social-media data, private participant records or large corpora are required. ACCESS for larger analysis or managed servers; retain institution-approved study tools. BA Game Design Branching narratives, rules and interaction prototypes, browser playtesting and published HTML games. A project needs a full 3D engine, large assets, persistent multiplayer servers or VR headsets. Campus engine / device labs; evaluate allocated GPU desktops and server support. BS Game Development Game logic, headless tests, small JavaScript games and browser-playable prototypes. Unity / Unreal desktop workflows, heavy 3D builds, rendering, VR or persistent multiplayer are required. Campus game / XR labs; ACCESS servers or GPU desktops after software and license checks. BA Information Science &amp; Arts Creative coding, interactive graphics, audio-visual sketches and web-based UX prototypes. Work needs high-end rendering, large media, installed creative software, XR devices or physical installations. Campus media / XR labs; allocated GPU or remote desktops where appropriate. MA Library &amp; Information Science Metadata cleaning, Python / R / SQL exercises, small text collections and static digital exhibits. A repository / ILS must run continuously, preservation storage must endure, or collections need access controls. Managed ACCESS teaching servers plus library systems; NAIRR Classroom for AI coursework. PhD Information Methods coursework, pilot analyses, reproducible examples and small model feasibility studies. Dissertation work needs sustained GPU time, large-scale simulation, controlled data or durable services. ACCESS / appropriate NAIRR research requests for research; Classroom only for eligible instruction. <p>Use the platform directory for quotas and the needs tool for a planning scenario. Course-specific software, accessibility and assessment requirements still need course-lead review.</p>","path":["Options by degree"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html","level":1,"title":"Four deliverables","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>For the College of Information Science Computing Resources Working Group. Point people: Barney Maccabe and Greg Chism, Faculty Director of Instructional Computing.</p> <p>Use these as working drafts. Each needs an agreed lead, source checks and group review; none is a completed college assessment or endorsed recommendation.</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html#1-resource-inventory","level":2,"title":"1. Resource inventory","text":"<p>Review the inventory · Download CSV</p> <p>Record hardware, software, data, cloud, personnel and support. For every entry, establish users, access, owner, funding, costs, limitations and the date checked. Keep “unknown” visible.</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html#2-needs-and-gaps-assessment","level":2,"title":"2. Needs and gaps assessment","text":"<p>Review the assessment framework · Prepare a needs brief · Blank needs register</p> <p>Gather current and anticipated research and classroom requirements, including student perspectives. Record barriers, impact, timing, supporting evidence and feasible alternatives. Summarize coverage and uncertainty before calling a gap college-wide.</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html#3-portfolio-of-approaches","level":2,"title":"3. Portfolio of approaches","text":"<p>Compare candidate approaches</p> <p>Evaluate better use of existing resources, shared services, collaboration, staffing models and new technology. Compare benefits, prerequisites, support, recurring obligations, accessibility and a small test that would inform the decision.</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html#4-recommendations","level":2,"title":"4. Recommendations","text":"<p>Review proposed actions · Recommendation template</p> <p>Separate actions the college can pursue from opportunities requiring university participation. For each: state evidence, benefit, owner role, dependencies, effort, funding status and a measure of success.</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"deliverables.html#milestones-and-review","level":2,"title":"Milestones and review","text":"<ul> <li>End of Fall 2026 semester: a preliminary draft of each deliverable.</li> <li>Early Spring 2027: refine and circulate within the college.</li> <li>University discussions: carry forward agreed college priorities when those discussions are scheduled.</li> </ul> <p>Milestones · Working-session guide · Download working packet</p>","path":["Four deliverables"],"tags":[]},{"location":"drafting-notes.html","level":1,"title":"Resource-request drafting notes","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Supporting assessment of the request tool. Start with the resource tool for use, or the report for the main findings.</p>","path":["Resource-request drafting notes"],"tags":[]},{"location":"drafting-notes.html#help-prepare-resource-requests","level":2,"title":"Help prepare resource requests","text":"<p>The needs tool offers a source-backed local template and an optional GPT-6 Luna rewrite after explicit consent for public/synthetic information. Luna uses a server-side credential and a shared allowance: 20 attempts/day, 200/month, five/network/day, up to 1,000 output tokens each. Attempts reserve $0.005 of the application allowance before calling the model, limiting this service to $1/month in reservations at current verified rates. This is separate from account-wide billing and hosting. Failed or timed-out calls are not retried automatically.</p> <p>For a manual campus route, use Arizona AI Assistant. Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI reports an AI Verde transition and $200/month of Bedrock credit, relayed by Greg Chism; review the credit boundaries and supplied model rates. U of A GenAI remains another documented route. Campus API integration and credit use have not been tested here.</p>","path":["Resource-request drafting notes"],"tags":[]},{"location":"drafting-notes.html#browser-model-assessment","level":3,"title":"Browser-model assessment","text":"<p>Browser inference works without API keys or per-request inference fees. We tested two quantized SmolLM2 models on synthetic examples in Chromium on this machine. The 135M model invented student-record details; a 360M model produced short drafts in about 9–13 seconds, but an integrated example added an unsupported participant condition and weakened unknown-hours language. Its initial model/runtime download was about 390 MB. These examples are a small feasibility check, not a general ranking of browser models.</p> <p>The integrated 360M worker generated with networking blocked after loading. That establishes local execution for that test; it does not establish reliable drafting. Browser inference remains disabled on the public site. The local template, optional hosted Luna draft and manual campus prompt are separate routes. The SmolLM2 model card also advises checking generated content. Preserve original quantities, source facts and unknowns in every draft.</p>","path":["Resource-request drafting notes"],"tags":[]},{"location":"drafting-notes.html#gpt-6-luna-drafting-test","level":3,"title":"GPT-6 Luna drafting test","text":"<p>On October 19, 2026, GPT-6 Luna completed three synthetic requests—data science instruction, MLIS data work and research software support—in 5.6–6.0 seconds each. Review against supplied briefs and dated inventory facts found that all three preserved unknown costs, staffing, permissions and capacity. Drafts were 135–163 words and kept questions for resource owners separate.</p> <p>Assessment: useful first drafts with human review. This was three examples with facts supplied, not a reliability guarantee or a matched comparison with browser models. Total usage was 4,157 input and 1,032 output tokens; estimated token charges were about $0.001, including reported cache writes, using published standard rates. This is an estimate, not a verified invoice. That initial test established no institutional approval or college operating budget. The subsequent optional integration has its own usage limits and deployment checks.</p>","path":["Resource-request drafting notes"],"tags":[]},{"location":"federal-compute.html","level":1,"title":"Federal platforms","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Federal support can fund machines, data systems, workflow services or operator teams. It does not automatically give every instructor an account or reserved GPUs. These 32 selected families overlap; this is not a machine census.</p> <p>Start with ACCESS for the proposed CPU + GPU classroom allocation. For research, match the work to batch pools, interactive services, data fabrics and mission-specific routes. OSPool’s opportunistic jobs differ from a live notebook service; PATh Facility credit eligibility differs from OSPool access.</p> <p>IDSS/MESA: an operator/service grant is not a generic teaching entitlement. Confirm the actual service, present support, eligible users and underlying provider policy. MESA funding alone does not establish PATh credit eligibility.</p> <p>Scope demand · Download map JSON · Full evidence</p> Search resourcesResource typeAll typescomputedatadomainenablerfundingidsstestbed<p>32 resources</p>computeACCESS<p>Broad CPU/GPU research and instruction; resource discovery and support.</p><p>Access: ACCESS project request; exchange credits for provider resources</p>Limits and sources<p>Provider quotas, scheduling, allocation term and software vary. Counts overlap with participating resource providers.</p><p>Funding: NSF-funded coordination + separately funded resource providers</p><p>ACCESS · national resource and support federation</p>computeOSG Open Science Pool (OSPool)<p>Independent text/image analyses, parameter sweeps, model evaluation and many short CPU/GPU jobs.</p><p>Access: Documented guest education notebook or facilitated full event accounts</p>Limits and sources<p>Guest four-hour timeout/files may be lost; full accounts usually end shortly after event. Interruptible fair share; complete/checkpoint jobs within 20 hours. No sensitive/proprietary data or classroom GPU reservation.</p><p>Funding: NSF PATh/IRIS-HEP + institutional/DOE in-kind contributions</p><p>OSG · consortium, funding and services · OSPool · opportunistic high-throughput capacity · OSPool · documented education access · OSPool · data and access-point rules</p>computePATh Facility<p>Dedicated throughput research jobs when the affiliated NSF program and credits are confirmed.</p><p>Access: Consult PATh; NSF-funded project startup/credit process</p>Limits and sources<p>IDSS is not explicitly listed in the published affiliated-program policy. MESA funding alone does not establish eligibility or course authorization; confirm with PATh/NSF. Separate from OSPool education access.</p><p>Funding: NSF PATh</p><p>PATh Facility · dedicated throughput credits · PATh · affiliated-program eligibility · PATh · research-credit request</p>computeNRP / Nautilus<p>Hosted teaching notebooks, GPUs, open-weight model APIs and containerized services.</p><p>Access: Institutional onboarding; hosted JupyterHub, API or project namespace</p>Limits and sources<p>Hosted hub starts with 5 GB homes; larger storage, class/GPU quota and hub-specific exceptions need approval. Ordinary-pod six-hour rules do not establish every hub limit. Not archival storage; idle volumes may be purged.</p><p>Funding: NSF-supported platform + participating institutions</p><p>National Research Platform / Nautilus · shared services · NRP · hosted open-weight model APIs · NRP · classroom products · NRP · hosted hub storage and login · NRP · workload and data lifecycle</p>computeJetstream2<p>Notebooks, RStudio, desktops, web/database teaching servers and vGPU environments.</p><p>Access: ACCESS allocation, course images and student/project access</p>Limits and sources<p>Always-on VM accounting, separate GPU exchange, concurrent quota and extra storage need approval. Independent backups required. Expiry/overdraw disables access and can destroy unresolved resources after thirty days.</p><p>Funding: NSF research-cloud infrastructure</p><p>Jetstream2 · teaching cloud through ACCESS · Jetstream2 · NSF research-cloud system · Jetstream2 · concurrent resource quotas · Jetstream2 · storage and backup responsibility · Jetstream2 · expiry and overdraw lifecycle</p>computeNAIRR / NAIRR Classroom<p>AI coursework, GPUs, model/data/tools and selected managed environments.</p><p>Access: Appropriate researcher or Classroom request</p>Limits and sources<p>US-based class eligibility and approved resources; instructor/TA support expected. Research and Classroom requests differ; partner offerings are not all federally purchased.</p><p>Funding: NSF-led federal partnership + public/private contributions</p><p>NAIRR Classroom · AI teaching resource request</p>computeCloudBank / CloudBank Classroom<p>Managed Python/R/C++ browser environments with login and student storage.</p><p>Access: General ACCESS Explore Classroom route; separate NAIRR Classroom route for AI coursework</p>Limits and sources<p>Managed CPU per-student limits/capacity need confirmation. Current registry says fall 2026 GPU pilot is at capacity; requests considered in 2027, not guaranteed. Renewal terms need reconciliation with education-period policy.</p><p>Funding: NSF support + awarded cloud budgets</p><p>CloudBank Classroom · managed teaching environment · CloudBank Classroom · general ACCESS teaching route · CloudBank Classroom · current GPU capacity record · CloudBank Classroom · support-document scope conflict</p>testbedChameleon / CHI@Edge<p>Systems/MLOps, edge/IoT, bare-metal experiments, specialized GPU/FPGA hardware.</p><p>Access: Eligible PI project; allocation and resource lease</p>Limits and sources<p>Experiment purpose, SU budget and short hardware leases. No unrestricted production hosting; student membership normally follows PI approval.</p><p>Funding: NSF-funded experimental infrastructure</p><p>Chameleon · funded experimental cloud testbed</p>testbedCloudLab<p>Distributed systems, reproducible root-access labs and networked application experiments.</p><p>Access: Approved project with planned class reservations</p>Limits and sources<p>Use only while actively experimenting; node data disappears at termination. Reserve for deadline bursts and export work.</p><p>Funding: NSF-funded testbed</p><p>CloudLab · classes and reservation boundaries · CloudLab · NSF-supported research facility</p>testbedFABRIC<p>Distributed AI, edge workloads, streaming/data movement and instrument-to-compute experiments.</p><p>Access: Project and experimental resource slices</p>Limits and sources<p>Experiment should need the network/testbed capabilities; not a stand-alone generic GPU account or production campus server.</p><p>Funding: NSF grants for research infrastructure</p><p>FABRIC · programmable network and edge testbed</p>domainNSF NCAR Derecho / analysis resources<p>Earth-system, environmental and geospatial data/AI instruction with consulting.</p><p>Access: Classroom/workshop or qualifying research allocation</p>Limits and sources<p>Earth-system mission fit; setup lead time, accounts, dates and reservations required. Separate grant not required for eligible classes.</p><p>Funding: NSF NCAR</p><p>NSF NCAR · domain-specific classroom allocations</p>domainDOE NERSC / ALCF / OLCF<p>Large mission-aligned scientific simulation, data analysis and AI research.</p><p>Access: ALCC, INCITE or facility-specific merit request</p>Limits and sources<p>ALCC does not require DOE sponsorship, but merit/mission/scaling requirements apply. Not the default for everyday college labs.</p><p>Funding: DOE Office of Science / ASCR</p><p>DOE ASCR · leadership computing allocation routes</p>domainNASA High-End Computing<p>NASA-aligned Earth/space/engineering computing.</p><p>Access: NASA-sponsored project / mission request process</p>Limits and sources<p>Sponsorship and mission/award review required; not a general open classroom resource.</p><p>Funding: NASA mission infrastructure</p><p>NASA HEC · sponsored mission-research resources</p>domainNIH AnVIL / Terra ecosystem<p>Genomic data-science notebooks, workflows and reproducible biomedical training.</p><p>Access: Project/data authorization plus cloud billing or awarded credits</p>Limits and sources<p>Free platform browsing does not mean free compute/storage/egress. NIH AnVIL differs from Purdue ACCESS Anvil; old credit calls are not current offers.</p><p>Funding: NIH NHGRI platform funding; cloud usage billed separately</p><p>NIH NHGRI AnVIL · domain cloud-analysis platform · AnVIL · execution and storage costs</p>computeCyVerse Discovery Environment / VICE<p>Python/R apps, data management and a practical UArizona course/pilot route.</p><p>Access: Basic account; confirm institutional Pro or collaboration access</p>Limits and sources<p>Basic 200 units/year, 5 GB, one job and no sharing/public links. Published UArizona free Pro is state-funded, not universal; GPU and class access need consultation.</p><p>Funding: Historical core NSF support; new project funding + subscriptions + Arizona TRIF</p><p>CyVerse · federal funding transition and UArizona state support</p>dataOpen Storage Network<p>Large actively used teaching/research datasets and portable object storage.</p><p>Access: Separate storage request through ACCESS; provider capacity</p>Limits and sources<p>Documented request range 10–50 TB; not CPU time, backup or archival permanence. Actual size/site capacity needs verification.</p><p>Funding: NSF seed support + participating storage operators</p><p>Open Storage Network · federal seed support · OSN · ACCESS storage allocation route · OSN · active-data scope</p>dataOSDF / Pelican<p>Deliver data to distributed jobs, notebooks and scientific applications.</p><p>Access: Connect existing store or allocated OSN/CC* storage through OSG</p>Limits and sources<p>Not another storage or compute award. Backing store/access policy required; not for highly regulated/PII/HIPAA data; preservation is separate.</p><p>Funding: OSG/PATh federal-supported services + data origins/caches</p><p>OSDF / Pelican · distribute data to computation · OSG · consortium, funding and services</p>idssMESA<p>Metadata, semantic discovery, reproducible data/agent workflows, Jupyter/R/desktop app layer.</p><p>Access: Published MESA portal/docs; underlying services govern compute</p>Limits and sources<p>Portal reads the existing CyVerse account/teams/quota; it does not add a second allowance. Selected apps, Save &amp; Exit, per-user Pro/TRIF entitlement, classroom/GPU capacity and support remain to be tested.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS; project site cites award 2632685</p><p>MESA · published data/AI tools and portal · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards · NSF IDSS · program and integration remit · MESA portal · existing CyVerse account and quota · MESA · application/resource selection · MESA · save and session lifecycle</p>idssNational Data Platform (NDP)<p>Discoverable compute-ready instructional data, reusable AI activities and capstone content.</p><p>Access: Public catalog/education activity; consult NDP for class workspace</p>Limits and sources<p>Some homepage features are forthcoming. Confirm available workspaces, quota, onboarding and underlying compute rather than equating an IDSS grant with a user allocation.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS</p><p>NDP · data discovery and education ecosystem · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>idssFabric for AI-Driven Science (FabAID)<p>Storage federation, cache/delivery, transformation, discovery and data-intensive workflows.</p><p>Access: Project service endpoints/consultation</p>Limits and sources<p>Data-service integration complements compute allocations; no universal GPU entitlement, class quota or archival guarantee established.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS</p><p>FabAID · data services fabric · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>idssInteractive Discovery Laboratory (iDLab)<p>Browser applications, interactive workspaces and cross-site data/workflow access.</p><p>Access: Project consultation; existing site account/allocations as required</p>Limits and sources<p>Participating sites retain policies/control. Gateway access is not an extra copy of the sites’ capacity or long-term archives; course readiness must be confirmed.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS</p><p>iDLab · interactive cross-site environment · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>idssBRIDGE National Center / Apache Texera<p>Visual/no-code workflows, reproducible social-science/data analyses and shared community models.</p><p>Access: Project collaboration or own supported platform deployment</p>Limits and sources<p>May require your own host/cloud/resources and operators. No verified no-cost public compute subscription or guaranteed class capacity.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS</p><p>BRIDGE · reusable data science workflows · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>idssNational Science Data Fabric (NSDF)<p>Standardized scientific data discovery/access, reproducibility and data/AI workforce development.</p><p>Access: Project contact; NSF announcement verifies expansion scope</p>Limits and sources<p>Funding/intent verified from NSF announcement; current user-specific access terms, compute quota and production readiness not established in this review.</p><p>Funding: NSF IDSS</p><p>NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>enablerOpen OnDemand + JupyterHub / BinderHub<p>Notebooks, RStudio, VS Code, remote desktops and visual/science gateways.</p><p>Access: Use provider-installed portal or deploy on approved compute</p>Limits and sources<p>Software is not hardware/time allocation. Only Open OnDemand’s funding is evidenced here; no claim that every JupyterHub installation is federally funded. Operators and backing resources remain necessary.</p><p>Funding: Open OnDemand federal-supported development; site funds operation</p><p>Open OnDemand · browser access to allocated computing · Open OnDemand · federal-supported software development · Jetstream2 · teaching cloud through ACCESS · National Research Platform / Nautilus · shared services</p>enablerPegasus / HTCondor / ACCESS Pegasus<p>Portable multi-step pipelines, task sweeps, data staging and transparent execution.</p><p>Access: Hosted ACCESS Pegasus with active allocation; OSG/PATh tools</p>Limits and sources<p>Uses existing allocations/pools; does not create another pool. Software portability is not data preservation or a substitute for operations.</p><p>Funding: NSF ACCESS/PATh-supported software and services</p><p>ACCESS Pegasus / HTCondor · workflow product layer · OSG · consortium, funding and services · PATh Facility · dedicated throughput credits</p>enablerCILogon / Globus<p>Campus login, role-based access, data transfer and cross-site workflow connections.</p><p>Access: Institution/provider identity configuration and endpoint support</p>Limits and sources<p>Basic services may be free while advanced support/sharing costs. Neither supplies a compute/storage allocation; CILogon says operations shifted to subscriptions in 2019.</p><p>Funding: Historical federal development/integration; current operations include subscriptions</p><p>CILogon · basic identity versus sustained paid operations · Globus · research transfer and subscription boundary</p>enablerScience gateways / SGX3<p>Usable domain applications and teaching interfaces beyond raw HPC command lines.</p><p>Access: Gateway consultation/community resources; project-specific gateway accounts</p>Limits and sources<p>Limited funded planning/UX/training differs from paid development/hosting/operations. A gateway catalog is not a universal free app-hosting plan.</p><p>Funding: NSF-funded SGX3; substantial operations can be client-funded</p><p>SGX3 / SGCI · gateway expertise and operations boundary</p>fundingIDSS program<p>National data-service development, transition to operations and planning.</p><p>Access: Institutional proposal / funded project collaboration</p>Limits and sources<p>Six named 2026 projects have different services/stages; the grant does not award every instructor CPU/GPU hours.</p><p>Funding: NSF</p><p>NSF IDSS · program and integration remit · NSF · six named 2026 IDSS awards</p>fundingCampus Cyberinfrastructure (CC*)<p>Campus compute, networking, data storage/digital archives and integration.</p><p>Access: Institutional funding process; actual local award/service rules</p>Limits and sources<p>Current page has no upcoming due dates and awaits new guidelines. Not an open national classroom account; sharing, operation and stewardship have to be funded.</p><p>Funding: NSF</p><p>NSF CC* · campus/regional infrastructure funding</p>fundingMajor Research Instrumentation (MRI)<p>Appropriate shared instrumentation and computing-linked research training.</p><p>Access: Institutional limited-submission/award process</p>Limits and sources<p>Current solicitation, instrument eligibility and campus proposal limits matter. A funding route is not a ready device/GPU account; this map does not assert an open submission window.</p><p>Funding: NSF</p><p>NSF MRI · research instrumentation funding</p>fundingState / Regional AI Infrastructure Hubs<p>Faculty course development, facilitators, shared AI-service coordination and regional scale.</p><p>Access: Institution/state/regional consortium; actual award and resource terms</p>Limits and sources<p>NSF does not acquire/fund the compute infrastructure under this solicitation. Partners must sustain it; no awarded Arizona hub is verified here.</p><p>Funding: NSF pays coordination/workforce/education; partners pay infrastructure</p><p>AI Infrastructure Hubs · NSF funds people, partners fund compute</p>fundingSCIPE / CyberTraining / ACCESS facilitation<p>Support ownership, instructor onboarding, reusable course materials and train-the-trainer capacity.</p><p>Access: Existing facilitator/training services or institutional grant pathway</p>Limits and sources<p>Grant programs do not promise staff for every instructor. Current call status and actual support agreements must be checked; college effort is still required.</p><p>Funding: NSF professional/training programs + service support</p><p>SCIPE · professional support workforce · CyberTraining · teaching and workforce development · ACCESS · national resource and support federation</p>&lt;p&gt;Search needs JavaScript; all resources are shown.&lt;/p&gt;","path":["Federal platforms"],"tags":[]},{"location":"gpu-benchmark.html","level":1,"title":"Run benchmarks","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Measure before piloting. Runnable DS and MLIS components exist; provider runs remain pending. Local synthetic controls are not AWS or CUDA evidence.</p> Component Run on Record CPU data-science and MLIS tests Already-authorized campus UITS/AWS, component testing only Wall time, configured threads, memory, input/output sizes and failures GPU DS and MLIS notebooks Kaggle or free Colab before piloting Actual device/VRAM, runtime, allocated memory, quota/session limits, failures and exports Target replay Awarded ACCESS CPU/GPU resource Equivalent outputs, actual architecture, provider accounting and concurrent behavior <p>Download runnable benchmark bundle · Benchmark result template</p>","path":["Run benchmarks"],"tags":[]},{"location":"gpu-benchmark.html#use-the-results","level":2,"title":"Use the results","text":"<ol> <li>Agree on inputs, configuration and correctness checks with each course lead.</li> <li>Run warm-up and retained repetitions. Preserve failed runs and platform labels.</li> <li>Export results, environment and hardware details.</li> <li>Use the included <code>size_access.py</code> and plan template for component demand. Add interactive idle time, provisioning, staff, storage, retries and concurrency without double counting.</li> <li>Confirm target-provider billing units. CPU core-hours and GPU device-hours are not automatically ACCESS credits.</li> </ol> <p>The MLIS GPU component is a projection/retrieval proxy; it does not establish pretrained semantic-retrieval or OCR quality. Validate those tasks separately if required.</p> <p>Explore a planning scenario · Prepare the ACCESS draft</p>","path":["Run benchmarks"],"tags":[]},{"location":"inventory.html","level":1,"title":"Resource inventory","text":"<p>Find a route for College of Information Science research or teaching. Filter by resource type and location, then open an entry for access, cost, limitations and dated sources.</p> <p>Download inventory CSV · Develop a resource request · Detailed platform references</p> Teaching on campus HPC: ask HPC Consult to confirm the current permitted route; published policy names retired Ocelote. Search inventoryResource typeAll typesCloud &amp; hostingCollaboration &amp; shared servicesData &amp; stewardshipHardware &amp; computeRSE &amp; personnelSoftware &amp; modelsSupport &amp; trainingWhere availableCollege, university and externalCollegeUniversityNational / external<p>33 entries</p>College · Owner confirmation neededCollege of Information Science IT support<p>Start with college technology support and escalation routes.</p><p>Contact: College office — infosci@arizona.edu; 520-621-3565 (routing contact, not a verified IT service desk)</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use the college IT support page; a public request procedure is not exposed in the page content. Ask the college to identify the current local IT owner.</p><p>Cost: No public fee schedule or support entitlement found.</p><p>Limits: Published IT page is sparse. Response hours, equipment coverage and escalation need confirmation.</p><p>Support: College office — infosci@arizona.edu; 520-621-3565 (routing contact, not a verified IT service desk)</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Inventory pendingCollege hardware, labs and loan equipment<p>Identify local equipment, lab access and any loan pool; capacity is not yet inventoried.</p><p>Contact: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Ask Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism to coordinate a roster of machines, locations, owners, access rules and any loan pool.</p><p>Cost: Unknown; inventory existing commitments and hours before proposing funding.</p><p>Limits: No shared college CPU/GPU pool, equipment checkout entitlement or spare capacity is verified.</p><p>Support: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p><p>Funding: To document; no new funding, staff time or loan inventory assumed.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Inventory question, not a verified service entitlement. Routing contact designated by the user; profile confirms role/email.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Inventory pendingCollege RSE and technical-support capacity<p>Identify engineering skills and support windows; available staff hours need confirmation.</p><p>Contact: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Ask Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism to identify technical roles, skills, appointments and realistic support windows.</p><p>Cost: Unknown; inventory existing commitments and hours before proposing funding.</p><p>Limits: No available FTE or support hours measured. Research programmers in a lab are not automatically a college-wide service.</p><p>Support: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p><p>Funding: To document; no new funding, staff time or loan inventory assumed.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Inventory question, not a verified service entitlement. Routing contact designated by the user; profile confirms role/email.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Inventory pendingCollege software, datasets and agreements<p>Clarify local software, datasets, agreements and renewal ownership.</p><p>Contact: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Ask the working group to identify license/data owners, storage, renewals and permitted research/classroom use.</p><p>Cost: Unknown; inventory existing commitments and hours before proposing funding.</p><p>Limits: No college license roster, API allowance or training/distribution rights verified.</p><p>Support: Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism — working-group point people</p><p>Funding: To document; no new funding, staff time or loan inventory assumed.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Inventory question, not a verified service entitlement. Routing contact designated by the user; profile confirms role/email.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Documented routeCollege research labs and collaborators<p>Find research capabilities and possible collaborators; shared access requires agreement.</p><p>Contact: College research-labs directory; Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism for working-group coordination</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Explore the college research-labs directory; approach the relevant lab lead for a research collaboration.</p><p>Cost: No general-use fee schedule or guaranteed shared access published.</p><p>Limits: A lab listing establishes research capability, not open compute, staff availability or a classroom reservation.</p><p>Support: College research-labs directory; Barney Maccabe or Greg Chism for working-group coordination</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Documented routeco/lab: Critical Archives &amp; Curation Collaborative<p>Explore critical archives, curation and community-centered data work.</p><p>Contact: Co-directors Jamie A. Lee and Zack Lischer-Katz — lab page</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Discuss digital curation, obsolete media/computers and digital-forensics collaborations with the co-directors.</p><p>Cost: Shared equipment access, project charges and supervision not published.</p><p>Limits: Research collaboration and preservation capability; no public equipment checkout policy, GPU entitlement or staffed digitization service.</p><p>Support: Co-directors Jamie A. Lee and Zack Lischer-Katz — lab page</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Documented routeXRG: Extended Reality &amp; Games Lab<p>Explore extended reality and games capabilities; access and equipment availability need confirmation.</p><p>Contact: Lila Boz and Ren Boz — lab leads listed on XRG page</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Discuss XR, virtual/augmented reality and interaction research with the lab leads.</p><p>Cost: No general-use equipment rate or access entitlement published.</p><p>Limits: Research lab, not a documented open GPU lab or college equipment-loan service; confirm scheduling and participation.</p><p>Support: Lila Boz and Ren Boz — lab leads listed on XRG page</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>College · Documented routeCollege course technology requirements<p>Check course software and device requirements before selecting a platform.</p><p>Contact: Course lead or faculty adviser; college office for routing</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Check the program-specific minimum hardware and operating-system requirements before designing an activity.</p><p>Cost: Student-owned device requirements; no provided machine or subsidy established.</p><p>Limits: Requirements vary by degree. Device specifications do not establish college-owned inventory or access to loan equipment.</p><p>Support: Course lead or faculty adviser; college office for routing</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeCampus HPC: research CPU and GPU<p>Research CPU and GPU computing; allocation, queue and data terms apply.</p><p>Contact: HPC Consult — ServiceNow ticket</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Eligible UArizona users register and join a faculty PI group. Published Puma standard allocation: 150,000 CPU-hours per PI group each month.</p><p>Cost: Standard allocation and consulting at no charge; GPU jobs use the same CPU-hour pool. Buy-in is a separate paid arrangement.</p><p>Limits: Shared, non-rollover allocation; queues and GPU memory constrain work. Instruction policy still names Ocelote only, although allocation notices say Ocelote retired Aug. 17, 2026. Teaching eligibility on current clusters is unresolved. Lynx/H200 specifications are published, but operating availability/allocation must be confirmed.</p><p>Support: HPC Consult — ServiceNow ticket</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗ · Source 4 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Research: confirm current hardware/accounting. Teaching: obtain HPC Consult confirmation of permitted cluster and terms before planning a class.</p>University · Conflicting documentationCampus HPC: classroom policy needs clarification<p>Confirm the current teaching route: published policy names a retired cluster.</p><p>Contact: HPC Consult — instructor should open a ticket</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Semester class groups are documented and share the faculty PI allocation. Ask HPC Consult for the currently permitted teaching cluster.</p><p>Cost: No separate class allocation; the faculty research pool is shared.</p><p>Limits: Policy restricts instruction to Ocelote, while current notices state Ocelote is decommissioned. Do not infer Puma/Lynx classroom permission. No expedited assignment jobs or individual student support promised.</p><p>Support: HPC Consult — instructor should open a ticket</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗ · Source 4 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Resolve the retired-cluster / instruction-policy conflict and confirm support and simultaneous class access in writing.</p>University · Documented routeHPC active storage and transfers<p>Active research storage and data transfers; not an independent backup.</p><p>Contact: HPC Consult — ServiceNow ticket</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: HPC account: 50 GB home; PI group: 500 GB communal space. Temporary xdisk on request: 200 GB–20 TB, up to 300 days.</p><p>Cost: Standard spaces supplied with account/group; rental is a separate priced service.</p><p>Limits: HPC data are not backed up; xdisk is temporary and node /tmp is cleared after a job. Storage quotas do not provide preservation or regulated-data approval.</p><p>Support: HPC Consult — ServiceNow ticket</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeHPC software, containers and shared model data<p>Find supported HPC software, containers and shared model data.</p><p>Contact: HPC Consult — software/setup questions</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use HPC modules, user environments and containers; shared datasets include Llama 2 and AlphaFold inputs.</p><p>Cost: Open-source tools may have no license charge; licensed products and model terms still apply. Compute uses the allocation.</p><p>Limits: Availability varies by module/version. Llama 2 has its own community license; model memory/storage requirements can be substantial. A software listing is not permission for every use.</p><p>Support: HPC Consult — software/setup questions</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗ · Source 4 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeHPC consulting and visualization support<p>Consult on HPC workflows and visualization; specialist capacity is not assumed.</p><p>Contact: HPC Consult ticket; visualization: vislab-consult@list.arizona.edu</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Open a ServiceNow ticket for onboarding, queue issues, code optimization and resource advice; request visualization help through its team.</p><p>Cost: HPC consulting is published as free of cost.</p><p>Limits: HPC team support hours: weekdays 8 a.m.–5 p.m., excluding university holidays. Consultation is not an assigned engineer or individualized student homework support.</p><p>Support: HPC Consult ticket; visualization: vislab-consult@list.arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · ORAI transition update; timing to confirmAI Verde → Arizona AI Assistant: planned transition<p>ORAI reports an Arizona AI Assistant transition and planned Bedrock credit; terms need confirmation.</p><p>Contact: Office of Responsible AI; current AI Verde contact Mithun Paul for migration questions</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Wolfgang Jetner at the Office of Responsible AI (ORAI), via Greg Chism, reports AI Verde will be sunset in favor of Arizona AI Assistant. Use the new portal to check sign-in, API access and enrollment; migration timing and eligibility need confirmation.</p><p>Cost: Planned $200/month of AWS Bedrock credit, reported by Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI and relayed by Greg Chism on October 19, 2026. Confirm who receives the allowance, renewal, covered usage and overage controls; not an unlimited compute or GPU-training allocation.</p><p>Limits: Supplied ORAI pricing screenshot lists 12 hosted models and per-token prices. These campus gateway rates differ from direct-provider rates. Credit is not transferred to this site’s OpenAI API; API compatibility, data rules, class concurrency and migration/export still need confirmation.</p><p>Support: Mithun Paul — mithunpaul@arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Campus Bedrock credit reported by Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI, via Greg Chism; allocation level and renewal terms to confirm.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — ORAI transition/credit update and pricing screenshot from Wolfgang Jetner, relayed by Greg Chism; portal HTTP 200, no signed-in entitlement test</p><p>Public university news describes Arizona AI Assistant as a fall pilot and AI Verde separately. The transition and $200/month credit come from Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI, relayed by Greg Chism; individual entitlements have not been tested. Pricing screenshot received October 19; its capture/effective date is unconfirmed. Confirm live account rates.</p><p>Next: Confirm transition date, account/credit scope, API base URL, permitted data, overage policy and exports with the Office of Responsible AI.</p>University · Documented routeU of A GenAI<p>Explore the documented NetID route for campus generative AI tools.</p><p>Contact: Responsible AI tools page and campus 24/7 IT support: 520-626-8324</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Active UArizona NetID; published FAQ describes limited early access. Check genai.arizona.edu for availability.</p><p>Cost: Published no-cost campus use.</p><p>Limits: Models/features may rotate; no guaranteed API, classroom concurrency or GPU-training allocation. Early-access conditions and data-specific exclusions apply; this differs from AI Verde.</p><p>Support: Responsible AI tools page and campus 24/7 IT support: 520-626-8324</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeCampus software licensing<p>Check campus software agreements, eligible users and license restrictions.</p><p>Contact: UITS 24/7 support — 520-626-8324</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use the UITS software catalog and student/faculty service routes; check each product and affiliation.</p><p>Cost: Student page lists Microsoft 365, Google Apps and Adobe Creative Cloud without added software charge while enrolled; other products are free or discounted. Student services are supported by the IT &amp; Library Fee.</p><p>Limits: Eligibility, device counts, renewal and commercial/research use vary. Listed MATLAB/Autodesk etc. do not imply every license or API credit is included.</p><p>Support: UITS 24/7 support — 520-626-8324</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeVCAT and Tech Zone software access<p>Explore remotely available or shared-space software; check application and class fit.</p><p>Contact: UITS 24/7 support — 520-626-8324</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: VCAT supplies virtual applications through a browser; Tech Zone labs provide installed software. Instructors can request a lab software installation.</p><p>Cost: Campus student-service route; fee-supported. Product-specific access and installation charges, if any, must be checked.</p><p>Limits: App/version and lab availability vary; not a general-purpose GPU allocation, unrestricted server or always-on research host.</p><p>Support: UITS 24/7 support — 520-626-8324</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeLibraries Data Cooperative: Python, R and ML support<p>Seek Python, R, machine-learning and research-data guidance.</p><p>Contact: Sarah Stueve — sarahstueve@arizona.edu (Python/ML); Jeff Oliver — jcoliver@arizona.edu (R/Git/visualization)</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Faculty, staff and students can consult library specialists on data science, visualization, programming and data management.</p><p>Cost: No project-specific fee or dedicated engineering allowance established by these pages.</p><p>Limits: Consultation, instruction and referrals; no dedicated developer hours, turnaround or ongoing operations commitment.</p><p>Support: Sarah Stueve — sarahstueve@arizona.edu (Python/ML); Jeff Oliver — jcoliver@arizona.edu (R/Git/visualization)</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeDigital scholarship and text/data mining<p>Explore digital scholarship, text analysis and data-mining support.</p><p>Contact: Heather Froehlich — froehlich@arizona.edu</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Consult about corpora, text analysis, humanities data, metadata and digital editing; listed open tools include OpenRefine, Voyant, AntConc and CollectionBuilder.</p><p>Cost: Listed tools are free to use; sustained project implementation/support not priced here.</p><p>Limits: Tool availability does not clear copyright, dataset licensing, hosting, compute or staff time.</p><p>Support: Heather Froehlich — froehlich@arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeLibrary datasets, databases and reuse rights<p>Check dataset access and reuse rights; library access does not imply redistribution rights.</p><p>Contact: Library Collections — LBRY-collectionmanagement@email.arizona.edu</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use licensed resources under current UA affiliation and the specific agreement. Contact Collections before TDM or use with AI.</p><p>Cost: Institutional subscriptions cover authorized access; additional TDM/API rights or fees depend on the agreement.</p><p>Limits: Reading access does not authorize bulk download, sharing, training or uploading to public AI tools. Each publisher agreement differs.</p><p>Support: Library Collections — LBRY-collectionmanagement@email.arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeGIS software, geospatial data and consultation<p>Find geospatial software, data and consultation.</p><p>Contact: Garrett Smith — garrettsmith@arizona.edu</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use the Libraries GIS/data guides and repository; discuss ArcGIS, QGIS, R and spatial analysis with a specialist.</p><p>Cost: QGIS is open source; ArcGIS eligibility and project-specific data/service terms require checking. No universal paid-product entitlement inferred.</p><p>Limits: Data licenses, service quotas and support capacity differ. A geospatial repository is not a general GPU or database-hosting award.</p><p>Support: Garrett Smith — garrettsmith@arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeReDATA: research data and code preservation<p>Assess research data and code deposits for preservation and reuse.</p><p>Contact: ReDATA team — data-management@arizona.edu</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Active faculty/staff, enrolled students and certain DCCs with valid NetID/library privileges can submit eligible final research materials for curatorial review.</p><p>Cost: FAQ: no cost to individual UArizona researchers; quota increases depend on need and availability.</p><p>Limits: Non-sensitive research materials and rights required; curatorial review mandatory. Not an active database/backup service. Published notice reports intermittent traffic-related errors and automated download restrictions; not a breach.</p><p>Support: ReDATA team — data-management@arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗ · Source 3 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Contact reconfirmation neededData Science Institute / CyVerse research software expertise<p>Explore research software expertise; published contact routes need reconfirmation.</p><p>Contact: Published DSI contact — RII-datascienceinstitute@arizona.edu (current operation to reconfirm)</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Indexed official DSI pages describe project/proposal consultation and cross-college teams; their live hostnames did not resolve during this review. Reconfirm the current route before relying on it.</p><p>Cost: No blanket free engineering-hour entitlement or public project rate established; scope and funding must be agreed.</p><p>Limits: Named engineering expertise is not an available appointment or staffed help desk for every course. Confirm capacity, deliverables, maintenance and funding.</p><p>Support: DSI — RII-datascienceinstitute@arizona.edu</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — indexed official text retrieved; direct live verification unsuccessful</p><p>Official-source search/fetch text supplied the contact and engineering roles, but direct checks of data7.arizona.edu and datascience.arizona.edu failed DNS resolution. This is an availability limitation, not evidence of service closure. No available staff hours verified.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeCCT Data Science: consultation and research software<p>Explore research data-science consultation and software support.</p><p>Contact: CCT Data Science team — current contact/consultation routes</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Drop-in/workshop routes and grant collaboration documented. The 80-hour Data Science Incubator is limited to ALVSCE faculty, staff and students.</p><p>Cost: Longer collaboration can use hired effort or a co-PI grant arrangement; Information Science eligibility for other services and rates must be confirmed.</p><p>Limits: The ALVSCE incubator is not an 80-hour entitlement for Information Science. Training/drop-in help differs from funded long-term engineering.</p><p>Support: CCT Data Science team — current contact/consultation routes</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeCATalyst Studios: XR, making and class partnerships<p>Explore making, XR and class partnerships; availability needs confirmation.</p><p>Contact: CATalyst Studios — contact and consultation booking</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Use Main Library makerspace hours, reserve eligible spaces/VR equipment, or plan a class visit with the team.</p><p>Cost: Free workshops are advertised; material/equipment-specific charges and eligibility must be checked.</p><p>Limits: Some machines require authorization classes. Reservations, supervision and supplies constrain access; no general-purpose compute/GPU reservation inferred.</p><p>Support: CATalyst Studios — contact and consultation booking</p><p>Funding: Institutional or project service; dedicated college capacity/funding not established.</p><p>Source 1 ↗ · Source 2 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19 — public documentation; no signed-in entitlement or owner confirmation</p><p>Access route, published cost basis and contact checked in public sources. Unpublished quotas, staffing and college capacity remain unknown.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, current availability, permitted use and support scope with this contact before relying on the resource.</p>University · Documented routeAI tools and data-use guidance<p>Check institutional AI-tool and data-use guidance before adoption.</p><p>Contact: Responsible AI campus support and current training organizers</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Review the current university decision table before selecting a tool.</p><p>Cost: Public guidance; project review effort must be scoped.</p><p>Limits: Data categories and account types matter; do not infer authorization from a vendor privacy claim.</p><p>Support: University AI, privacy and relevant data owners.</p><p>Funding: University guidance; not a compute or staff allocation.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19</p><p>Published guidance/training route only; not a compute or staff allocation.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>University · Documented routeCampus AI learning and events<p>Find learning opportunities and training; events do not establish ongoing support.</p><p>Contact: Responsible AI campus support and current training organizers</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Follow the university AI resources page for training and event routes.</p><p>Cost: Check each offering; staff learning time is still required.</p><p>Limits: A course/event is not staffed project support or a reserved technical service.</p><p>Support: University AI learning and event organizers.</p><p>Funding: University initiative; event availability varies.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: 2026-10-19</p><p>Published guidance/training route only; not a compute or staff allocation.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Application / approvalNSF ACCESS<p>Request project-based CPU and GPU resources; an award and provider fit are required.</p><p>Contact: ACCESS support and selected resource provider</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Match the project and teaching/research use to a resource and request.</p><p>Cost: Allocated resource access is conditional; operations/support remain separate.</p><p>Limits: No award, class reservation or provider benchmark established.</p><p>Support: ACCESS support plus the selected resource provider.</p><p>Funding: Federally supported allocation route.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; not newly rechecked</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Application / approvalNAIRR Classroom<p>Explore eligible instructor-led AI classroom pilots; scope and resources are conditional.</p><p>Contact: NAIRR program / current Classroom call</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Check the current instructor/course call and resource terms.</p><p>Cost: Conditional award/resource terms; not funding in hand.</p><p>Limits: An eligible AI pilot does not supply every course or its ongoing staff support.</p><p>Support: Program and resource-specific support.</p><p>Funding: Federal pilot opportunity.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; recheck call before use</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Documented routeOSPool / throughput computing<p>Assess opportunistic throughput jobs; this is a different service model from live class notebooks.</p><p>Contact: OSPool facilitation / support</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Assess independent, checkpointed research jobs and facilitated onboarding.</p><p>Cost: Opportunistic use subject to policy and available capacity.</p><p>Limits: Interruptions and queue behavior differ from live classroom sessions.</p><p>Support: OSG/OSPool support route.</p><p>Funding: Federally supported/shared infrastructure.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; recheck before use</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Project / service termsMESA / CyVerse collaboration<p>Explore compatible data-service collaboration; project funding is not a general compute entitlement.</p><p>Contact: MESA project team / underlying service provider</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Explore public service and collaboration routes; confirm underlying access.</p><p>Cost: Service-specific; no free college compute entitlement inferred.</p><p>Limits: Grant participation and advertised products do not establish approved class capacity.</p><p>Support: Project/platform maintainers; commitments to confirm.</p><p>Funding: IDSS/project and underlying service support differ.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; recheck before use</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Bounded / conditionalFree GPU and cloud-credit experiments<p>Use bounded GPU notebooks or credits for preliminary experiments; quotas and hardware vary.</p><p>Contact: Provider-specific help routes in comparison</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Compare Kaggle, free Colab and credit-based platforms in the existing directory.</p><p>Cost: Quotas, cards and paid conversion vary.</p><p>Limits: Useful for bounded experiments; not a guaranteed full-term GPU reservation.</p><p>Support: Provider/community support; no class support commitment.</p><p>Funding: Provider-sponsored tiers or promotional credits.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; recheck before use</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>National / external · Service-specificRepositories, databases and preservation<p>Separate code hosting, active databases, scholarly deposits and preservation needs.</p><p>Contact: Service-specific contacts in continuity plan</p>Access, costs, limitations and sources<p>Access: Match code, active databases, research deposits and archival needs separately.</p><p>Cost: Storage, activity and hosting limits vary.</p><p>Limits: Compute allocations do not automatically provide durable databases or preservation.</p><p>Support: Institutional librarians, data owners and hosting operators.</p><p>Funding: Institutional and provider arrangements differ.</p><p>Source 1 ↗</p><p>Review basis: Earlier compute-ledger review; recheck before use</p><p>Earlier source-ledger review; current resource-owner access, cost and contact not newly verified in this campus expansion.</p><p>Next: Confirm eligibility, available capacity, support commitment and funding with the resource owner.</p>&lt;p&gt;All entries are shown; filtering requires JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;","path":["Resources"],"tags":[]},{"location":"inventory.html#keep-the-inventory-useful","level":2,"title":"Keep the inventory useful","text":"<p>Confirm access, owner, support window, funding, costs and limits before changing an entry to verified. Record missing resources as unknown, not unavailable. Use the detailed platform directory for further comparisons.</p>","path":["Resources"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs-assessment.html","level":1,"title":"Needs and gaps assessment","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Draft framework — inputs not yet collected by this site. The resource inventory and historical provider changes identify questions to examine; they do not establish the size or distribution of college needs.</p> <p>Describe a need · Download blank needs register</p>","path":["Needs and gaps assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs-assessment.html#questions-to-investigate","level":2,"title":"Questions to investigate","text":"Area What we need to learn Useful evidence Compute and cloud Which activities need local devices, interactive sessions, batch jobs, GPUs or hosted models? Small measured workloads, access delays and required concurrency Software and models Which licenses, APIs, environments and accessible interfaces are required? Current agreements, use cases, compatibility and renewal terms Data Which datasets, rights, metadata, storage and preservation services are missing? Data inventories, permissions, stewardship and export/restore needs RSEs and support Which skills and support windows are required, and what capacity is actually available? Task estimates, existing commitments and service-owner confirmation Research and instruction How do research schedules, class deadlines and student access differ? Course/project scenarios and faculty/student perspectives Collaboration What can be shared or improved before adding infrastructure? Underused resources, compatible workflows and willing partners","path":["Needs and gaps assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs-assessment.html#assess-a-reported-gap","level":2,"title":"Assess a reported gap","text":"<p>Distinguish not available, not accessible, not known about, not supported, not suitable and not affordable. They require different responses.</p> <p>For each need, capture who is affected, the activity being blocked, current workaround, timing and evidence. Compare it with a verified resource route. A technical shortage should be distinguished from permissions, onboarding, skills or support barriers.</p>","path":["Needs and gaps assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs-assessment.html#summarize-responsibly","level":2,"title":"Summarize responsibly","text":"<p>Report which parts of the college and which roles contributed, the number of usable inputs and important missing perspectives. Keep measured demand, self-reported needs, estimates and unanswered questions separate. Do not scale a few examples into college-wide GPU, spending or staffing totals.</p>","path":["Needs and gaps assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs-assessment.html#ready-for-a-preliminary-draft-when","level":2,"title":"Ready for a preliminary draft when…","text":"<p>The group has a versioned needs register, a documented account of participation, significant gaps linked to evidence, and a clear list of remaining unknowns. Refine these with faculty, students and resource owners early in Spring 2027.</p>","path":["Needs and gaps assessment"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs.html","level":1,"title":"Resource request tool","text":"<p>Describe the research or teaching activity, the resources that would help and the barriers. You do not need a GPU-hour estimate or a budget. Unknown quantities can stay unknown.</p> <p>Local by default. Preparing a brief does not submit it to the working group. Optional Luna drafting sends a reviewed public/synthetic brief for AI processing only with your consent. Keep identifying details, student records and confidential research out of this public tool.</p> Try a software/data exampleTry a classroom example What are you trying to do? (required)What resources would help? (choose at least one)Hardware / computeSoftware / modelsData / stewardshipCloud / hostingRSE / engineeringTechnical support / trainingCollaboration / shared servicesActivity typeResearchInstructionBoth research and instructionCurrent or anticipatedCurrent needAnticipated / emerging needWhen neededThis semesterEarly Spring 2027Within 12 monthsNot yet knownImpactWork is blockedWork is limited or difficultExploratory opportunityData involvedNot yet knownPublic / syntheticInternal / student recordsRestricted / regulatedFunding positionNot yet knownNo funds identifiedExisting resources identified; terms to verifyConfirmed funding; permitted use to verifyWhat is getting in the way?Access / eligibilityCapacity / reliabilityCosts / fundingSoftware / data rightsSkills / onboardingStaff / support timeNot yet knownOptional detail · leave unknown quantities blankPeople or independent groupsPotential support hours/weekCollaboration opportunityNot yet knownWithin the collegeAcross the universityExternal partner / shared infrastructureSoftware, models, data or environment requirementsRSE skills, technical support or trainingExisting resource or workaroundPrepare my briefClear fields <p></p> <p>Prepare a local brief. Nothing is sent unless you choose Luna drafting.</p>","path":["Resource tool"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs.html#workingResultsTitle","level":2,"title":"Your local needs brief","text":"","path":["Resource tool"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs.html#workingActivity","level":3,"title":"Next steps to reviewOptional Luna draft","text":"<p>Barriers: </p><p></p>Download briefDownload JSON<p>Share the download through the group’s agreed university channel. No working-group submission channel is configured on this public site.</p>Develop a resource request<p>This local template turns your brief into a reviewable request and adds documented resource routes. It does not use an LLM or decide eligibility. Candidate routes need owner confirmation.</p>Request draftDownload request draftDownload AI drafting promptCopy AI drafting prompt<p></p><p>Use GPT-6 Luna to rewrite your brief and the dated resource facts into a short first draft. Review the result yourself. The local template above is always available.</p> I reviewed my brief. It contains only public/synthetic, non-identifying information, and I agree to send it and the listed resource facts through the drafting service to OpenAI.<p>Limited pilot: five attempts per network each day. Your local template remains available.</p>Usage limits and data handling<p>Shared allowance: 20 attempts/day, 200/month, up to 1,000 output tokens each. Failed attempts count; there are no automatic retries. The application keeps allowance counters and rotating network hashes, not your brief or generated text. OpenAI processes the requested text under its API terms; store:false is requested, not a zero-retention guarantee.</p>Draft with Luna<p></p>AI-assisted draft — review and edit before sharingDownload reviewed draft<p>Campus alternative: copy or download the public/synthetic prompt and paste it into Arizona AI Assistant ↗. Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI, via Greg Chism, reports that AI Verde will transition to this service with $200/month of Bedrock credit available; credit scope, eligibility and renewal terms need confirmation. Transition and model pricing →. U of A GenAI is another documented NetID route. The site does not connect to either campus service.</p>","path":["Resource tool"],"tags":[]},{"location":"needs.html#if-technical-sizing-would-help","level":2,"title":"If technical sizing would help","text":"<p>Use the optional compute estimator for CPU/GPU, concurrency and storage assumptions. It is one attachment to the need, not a requirement for participating. Measured demand and provider access still need confirmation.</p> &lt;p class=\"guide-note\"&gt;Local brief generation needs JavaScript. You can still use the &lt;a href=\"computing-needs-register.csv\"&gt;blank needs register&lt;/a&gt; to record the activity, resource types, barriers, timing and unknowns, then share it through the agreed university channel.&lt;/p&gt; <p>How needs will be assessed · Working-group deliverables</p>","path":["Resource tool"],"tags":[]},{"location":"pilot-plan.html","level":1,"title":"Two-course pilot","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Proposed candidates: INFO 521 and LIS 671. Model 40 students each over 15 weeks. Term, offering, instructor agreement and actual enrollment remain unconfirmed.</p> <p>Test components → size ACCESS CPU + GPU → replay on the target → ten-person canary → full-class rehearsal → teach → export and review.</p>","path":["Two-course pilot"],"tags":[]},{"location":"pilot-plan.html#info-521-introduction-to-machine-learning","level":2,"title":"INFO 521: Introduction to Machine Learning","text":"<p>Build a reproducible data-cleaning and exploratory-analysis notebook, then fit a small CPU model with held-out validation. Explain missingness, leakage, uncertainty and data provenance.</p> <p>Inputs: Generate a synthetic civic-service dataset with no real residents; keep core CSV inputs below 250 MB and test peak memory against the awarded workspace. Real/public datasets require a rights and data-classification check.</p> <p>Environment: Requested: Python, JupyterLab, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib and an approved reproducible package manifest; R/RStudio only if the course requires it.</p> Outputs and acceptance<ul><li>Run the same environment from a browser and from the CPU fallback.</li><li>Produce a reproducible notebook with an environment manifest and checksummed inputs.</li><li>Evaluate a small model without requiring a GPU or a paid student account.</li></ul><p>Run the whole assignment from a fresh student workspace; require execution without out-of-memory errors at the approved spec, reproducible key outputs and a complete exported submission.</p>","path":["Two-course pilot"],"tags":[]},{"location":"pilot-plan.html#lis-671-digital-curation-and-digital-preservation","level":2,"title":"LIS 671: Digital Curation and Digital Preservation","text":"<p>Audit a collection catalog, normalize names/dates/identifiers, document a metadata crosswalk, query an embedded catalog database and export a preservation-ready package with README, schema, provenance and checksums.</p> <p>Inputs: Use a synthetic 10,000–100,000-record collection catalog with Dublin Core-style fields. Add public/licensed records only after rights review; exclude patron, circulation, donor and restricted collection records.</p> <p>Environment: Requested: JupyterLab/Python, CSV/JSON/XML parsing and embedded SQLite; optional MARC parsing packages after approval. OpenRefine or other hosted applications require a separate provider fit check.</p> Outputs and acceptance<ul><li>Explain how transformations change discoverability and preserve provenance.</li><li>Validate a metadata crosswalk and query structured collection records.</li><li>Export usable CSV/JSON/XML records and describe a suitable repository/retention route.</li></ul><p>Execute the complete workflow in a fresh workspace; validate schema, record counts, identifiers and query results; reopen the exported package using the fallback environment.</p>","path":["Two-course pilot"],"tags":[]},{"location":"pilot-plan.html#people-and-support","level":2,"title":"People and support","text":"<p>Greg’s ten weekly hours cover all his work, including EduCloud. Two course support TAs and course leads have separate potential hours; course leads need no salary. A distinct independent backup is still unfilled. Do not promise a support window until commitments exist.</p> <p>Use small-data CPU alternatives and assignment accommodations if GPU access fails. Keep rosters/grades in approved institutional systems. Test backups and exports before required use.</p> <p>Hours and grant dependencies · Admission checks · Full plan text · Existing pilot brief PDF</p> <p>The PDF retains its October 12 scope; the web guide is the current navigation and planning summary.</p>","path":["Two-course pilot"],"tags":[]},{"location":"pilot-stages.html","level":1,"title":"Stages &amp; milestones","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>Current focus: provider benchmarks. Dates are relative to an approved future term. Six active preparation weeks do not include unpredictable allocation-review waiting time.</p> 01Before piloting · preparation weeks 1–2Test components and benchmark GPUs<p>Measure the DS and MLIS components before sizing the classroom allocation.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>Agree the exact inputs, workload configuration and correctness checks with course leads.</li><li>Run CPU components on already-authorized campus UITS/AWS; record runtime, memory, files and failures.</li><li>Run the GPU notebooks on Kaggle or free Colab; export results and actual device/quota information.</li></ul><p>Advance when: Provider measurements are usable and the approved workload envelope is documented. Local CPU controls do not count as AWS or CUDA performance evidence.</p> 02After benchmarks · before canarySubmit a sized ACCESS CPU + GPU request<p>Request the large CPU and GPU allocation with resource-specific quantities.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>Confirm course/term/cohort scope, designated PI, syllabi and required attachments.</li><li>Submit the measured ACCESS request; record submission and review as distinct events.</li><li>Confirm the award, CPU/GPU exchanges, storage and concurrency limits; CloudBank is conditional if its managed service fits.</li></ul><p>Advance when: The provider-approved CPU/GPU configuration and educational project period are known. Review time may extend preparation; an application is not an award.</p> 03After award · preparation weeks 3–4Run a ten-person component canary<p>Replay on ACCESS, then test one assignment per course with a small group.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>Replay representative workloads on the awarded CPU/GPU architecture and revise resource estimates.</li><li>Run a ten-volunteer component canary, five per proposed course, with synthetic inputs.</li><li>Confirm saved-file restart, portable exports and support effort; if using Classroom 50, rehearse assignment-to-grade flow and separate Codespaces/Actions limits.</li></ul><p>Advance when: Both workflows succeed and unresolved defects are recorded. Narrow or defer if core fit fails.</p> 04After canary · preparation weeks 5–6Rehearse the whole class and recovery<p>Demonstrate the whole-class workload and independent recovery before required use.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>With provider permission, rehearse the planned class load and account isolation.</li><li>Verify submissions, accessibility, independent restore, outage fallback and staffed support.</li><li>Review all thirteen applicable gates; confirm any necessary grant-funded needs and distinct backup coverage.</li></ul><p>Advance when: Readiness has dated evidence and named owners. Greg’s work stays within ten hours/week; there is no cash budget or assumed grant award.</p> 05Weeks 1–15Teach the two-course pilot<p>Run the bounded two-course pilot on ACCESS with regular reviews.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>Orient learners, publish feasible support windows and retain equivalent CPU-assessed activities.</li><li>Review access, resource usage, support and learner evidence at teaching weeks 3, 8 and 12.</li><li>Track allocation expiry alerts at 60, 30, 14 and 7 days; decide the continuation route at least 30 days before expiry.</li></ul><p>Advance when: The workload and support remain inside confirmed capacity; pause a failing component and use the rehearsed fallback.</p> 06Term end + two weeksExport, close and decide<p>Close the teaching cycle with usable exports and a deliberate next-term decision.</p>Events and gate<ul><li>Export learner work and reconcile grade records before access ends.</li><li>Verify approved preservation copies and independent recovery; apply retention, account closure and deletion rules.</li><li>Review actual resource/support demand and educational evidence; confirm a new project or approved continuation process.</li></ul><p>Advance when: Exports and closure are verified. Educational allocation extension is not assumed.</p> <p>All milestones after preparation are proposed. No award, resource reservation, course term or classroom admission is confirmed. Review the 13 checks →</p>","path":["Stages &amp; milestones"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html","level":1,"title":"Primers for the working group","text":"<p>Six starting points for discussing computing resources across the College of Information Science. Use the questions to identify evidence and a useful next step.</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#1-start-with-the-activity","level":2,"title":"1. Start with the activity","text":"<p>An interactive class, a batch research job, a hosted model and a public website need different services. A GPU is one component; memory, software, storage, accounts and support can be the actual constraint.</p> <p>Discuss: What must people be able to do, when, and with how many simultaneous users?</p> <p>Develop a brief · Optional technical estimate</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#2-separate-availability-from-dependable-access","level":2,"title":"2. Separate availability from dependable access","text":"<p>A free account or published machine specification does not reserve capacity or authorize classroom use. Research tolerates some queues; a scheduled class may not. Campus HPC teaching policy still names retired Ocelote, so the current permitted teaching route needs clarification.</p> <p>Discuss: Who is eligible, what is guaranteed, and what happens when access fails?</p> <p>Resource inventory · Classroom checks</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#3-count-the-people-and-recurring-work","level":2,"title":"3. Count the people and recurring work","text":"<p>Allocations and credits do not automatically supply research software engineers (RSEs), onboarding, accessible interfaces, course support or a backup operator. Estimate tasks and hours before proposing a staffing or funding model.</p> <p>Discuss: Who maintains the service, supports users and handles renewals and recovery?</p> <p>Candidate approaches · Pilot hours and grant dependencies</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#4-treat-data-software-and-preservation-separately","level":2,"title":"4. Treat data, software and preservation separately","text":"<p>Code repositories, licensed datasets, active storage, databases and preservation repositories have different purposes. Check rights, permitted data, exports and restore. A working copy is not an independent backup or a scholarly archive.</p> <p>Discuss: What may be stored or shared, who owns it, and how will it outlast the account?</p> <p>Repositories, hosting and data · Campus data and software services</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#5-compare-routes-by-the-whole-commitment","level":2,"title":"5. Compare routes by the whole commitment","text":"<p>Use existing campus services, shared infrastructure, efficient workflows and national allocations where they fit. Free tiers and promotional credits can support bounded experiments; an operating service also needs ownership, support and continuity.</p> <p>Discuss: What small, reversible test would distinguish the promising options?</p> <p>Federal platforms · Cloud credits and limits · Classroom 50</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#6-keep-the-evidence-proportional","level":2,"title":"6. Keep the evidence proportional","text":"<p>A self-reported need, an estimate, a benchmark and an owner-confirmed commitment are different evidence. A few examples cannot establish college-wide demand, savings or available staffing.</p> <p>Discuss: Whose perspective is missing, what is measured, and which claims still need confirmation?</p> <p>Needs assessment framework · Report and limitations</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"primers.html#a-useful-output-from-a-discussion","level":2,"title":"A useful output from a discussion","text":"<p>Record the activity → the barrier → a route to investigate → a person who can confirm it → the next useful test. Keep unknowns visible. The working materials provide the registers and review framework.</p>","path":["Primers"],"tags":[]},{"location":"publishing.html","level":1,"title":"Publishing options","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Decision: use Zensical for this guide and keep GitHub Pages hosting. It supplies search and consistent navigation; the needs tool remains a small browser-only component. No paid platform or new account is needed.</p>","path":["Publishing options"],"tags":[]},{"location":"publishing.html#what-we-compared","level":2,"title":"What we compared","text":"Option Best use Tradeoff for this site Zensical — selected Resource guides with search, sidebars and Markdown content Python-based workflow fits the existing builders. Custom CSS/JS is supported. Pin the version and check compatibility before upgrades. Astro Starlight Documentation with richer custom components Built-in search and accessible navigation; strong component flexibility. Adds a Node/Astro content and build workflow. Quarto Reports, notebooks and teaching materials published together Good scientific authoring, navigation and search. Adds the Quarto toolchain; useful if executable reports become central. Custom HTML, as before A distinctive small landing page Maximum control, but navigation, search and page consistency require ongoing custom work. <p>These are publishing engines, not compute providers. Any can produce static files for GitHub Pages. Moving the authoring system does not create teaching compute or durable institutional ownership of the hosting service.</p>","path":["Publishing options"],"tags":[]},{"location":"publishing.html#design-reference","level":2,"title":"Design reference","text":"<p>MESA’s public site uses direct headings, open space, simple links and short sections. Its landing page links to a separate documentation area. This guide adopts that clarity while keeping Arizona navy/red accents, searchable resources and a consistent page structure. It is not a copy of MESA or a claim of institutional service approval.</p>","path":["Publishing options"],"tags":[]},{"location":"publishing.html#maintenance","level":2,"title":"Maintenance","text":"<ul> <li>Build with Zensical 0.0.71, pinned for this release; published files stay in the existing repository.</li> <li>Keep factual sources in the evidence ledger and the public guide concise.</li> <li>Preserve existing page addresses, downloads and the five-slide talk.</li> <li>Test search, mobile navigation and the needs tool before upgrading. Zensical is currently a 0.x product; unsupported MkDocs plugins are not automatically compatible.</li> <li>Built-in client-side search requires JavaScript. Core information and links remain readable without it.</li> </ul>","path":["Publishing options"],"tags":[]},{"location":"publishing.html#primary-references","level":2,"title":"Primary references","text":"<p>Reviewed October 19, 2026. This is a site-fit assessment, not a performance benchmark.</p> <ul> <li>Zensical installation · custom JavaScript/CSS · search · GitHub Pages publishing</li> <li>Zensical compatibility and migration · supported plugins</li> <li>Astro Starlight features</li> <li>Quarto websites and publishing</li> </ul>","path":["Publishing options"],"tags":[]},{"location":"readiness.html","level":1,"title":"Before required classroom use","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> Supporting compute pilot. These DS/MLIS proposals retain their own $0 cash and Greg’s ten-hour weekly envelope. They are not college-wide capacity findings or the working group’s selected solution. Return to working-group deliverables → <p>All 13 admission checks are planned, not run. A working notebook or award alone does not establish classroom readiness. Open a check to see the evidence and stop condition.</p> 1. Operations · scope<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Confirm eligible PI, actual courses/leads, Greg’s ten-hour/week total envelope, separate TA/lead hours, distinct backup and full-term coverage. No cash budget; fund any necessary expenses only from a confirmed eligible grant.</p><p>Owner / timing: Greg + course leads / Before request</p><p>Evidence: Named owners, realistic hours log, separate support commitments and confirmed grant/allowable-cost evidence if paid resources are needed.</p><p>Stop if: Counting ten hours per role, assuming grant award, missing backup, or unfunded necessary spending.</p> 2. Teaching · benchmark<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Complete campus AWS CPU component tests and free-resource GPU benchmarks; retain failures, correctness, measured memory/runtime, checkpoint/export results and workload scope. Reproduce representative GPU work on awarded ACCESS hardware before class.</p><p>Owner / timing: Greg (operator) + course leads / Before student canary / request sizing</p><p>Evidence: Versioned workload/environment manifest, dataset checksums, dated result rows, actual GPU/account limits and target-resource comparison. No measurements recorded yet.</p><p>Stop if: Unmeasured GPU amounts, treating free quota as guaranteed classroom capacity, or assuming benchmark timings transfer unchanged to ACCESS.</p> 3. Access · provider<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Confirm ACCESS CPU/GPU allocation, separate resource exchanges, actual quotas/storage, course dates and project structure; campus AWS stays test-only. If CloudBank is selected, confirm service type, CPU/GPU availability and accounting.</p><p>Owner / timing: Allocation lead + selected ACCESS provider / Before request / gate to class</p><p>Evidence: Provider response and approved award/resource configuration, recorded privately.</p><p>Stop if: Assuming unspecified limits, general broker terms or automatic renewal.</p> 4. Access · accounts<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Every approved student/staff test account authenticates; student A cannot read/write student B files or instructor grading areas; removal/revocation works.</p><p>Owner / timing: Operator + course leads / Canary; repeat before week 1</p><p>Evidence: Access matrix and dated test results; no passwords or roster identifiers in public reports.</p><p>Stop if: Access leakage, shared credentials or unverified removal.</p> 5. Teaching · software<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Both complete representative assignments run on the approved CPU image from fresh workspaces; no paid account or GPU is required. Pin the approved environment and input checksums.</p><p>Owner / timing: Each course lead / Canary</p><p>Evidence: Clean-run outputs, environment manifest, resource measurements and a known-good baseline.</p><p>Stop if: Missing packages, memory failure, unapproved app or required GPU dependency.</p> 6. Access · capacity<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>With provider permission, rehearse 80 simultaneous students plus agreed staff use in three representative sessions. Proposed p95 login-to-workspace ≤5 minutes; no unresolved assignment-blocking launch failure.</p><p>Owner / timing: Operator + provider / 2–4 weeks before term</p><p>Evidence: Dated latency distribution, workspace success count, quota/usage forecast and provider-agreed concurrency.</p><p>Stop if: No permission/capacity evidence; peak spec exceeds awarded limits.</p> 7. Teaching · save<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Edit, save, stop/restart and recover a notebook/data package; verify filesystem location and retention. Export and re-import it into the CPU fallback.</p><p>Owner / timing: Course leads + TAs / Canary; repeat after image update</p><p>Evidence: Saved-file checksums, restart/export receipt and fresh-environment reopen.</p><p>Stop if: Student work depends on an unsaved session or cannot be exported.</p> 8. Teaching · grades<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Distribute a versioned assignment, collect it, reconcile all test submissions, preserve student authorship and instructor judgment, and import/export a test grade receipt through the approved LMS workflow. If Classroom 50 is selected, verify Team/Enterprise organization eligibility, permissions, billing controls and an independent export; Codespaces and Actions quotas remain separate.</p><p>Owner / timing: Course leads / Before graded use</p><p>Evidence: Submission manifest and grade reconciliation results in the approved institutional destination. Classroom 50 canary and quota/fallback records if selected; no additional Codespaces allocation assumed.</p><p>Stop if: A leaderboard/auto-score substitutes for the authoritative grade, or a missing receipt goes undetected.</p> 9. Operations · data<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Approve provider and backup data destinations, lawful/appropriate datasets, access roles and retention. Keep grades/rosters in the approved LMS/institutional systems; publish only aggregate results.</p><p>Owner / timing: IT/privacy reviewer + Libraries / Before student data</p><p>Evidence: Approved data map, rights notes, retention schedule and service/accessibility review.</p><p>Stop if: Assuming research storage or this public site is approved for private student records.</p> 10. Teaching · accessibility<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Rehearse keyboard and screen-reader access, readable instructions and equivalent small-data CPU/offline activity; resolve barriers in essential workflows.</p><p>Owner / timing: Greg (design coordination) + course leads / Before course admission</p><p>Evidence: User/task review and course-lead sign-off, with accommodations and fallback instructions.</p><p>Stop if: An essential assessed activity is inaccessible without an equivalent route.</p> 11. Recovery · recovery<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Make independent copies in approved destinations and restore onto a different host/environment. Proposed RPO ≤24 hours and RTO ≤one business day; measure both.</p><p>Owner / timing: Primary + backup operators / Before course admission</p><p>Evidence: Restore logs, independent file checksums, recovery timing and backup-operator runbook rehearsal.</p><p>Stop if: No independent copy or unmeasured recovery; relying only on snapshots or cold archival retrieval.</p> 12. Operations · support<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Publish actual staffed help windows and escalation route; rehearse an outage with CPU/small-data fallback. Proposed first response within one staffed business day; do not promise 24/7 support. Keep actual Greg hours within ten/week across all duties; reduce scope when demand exceeds coverage.</p><p>Owner / timing: Owner + TAs + operators / Before week 1; weekly</p><p>Evidence: Coverage schedule, ticket routing, outage exercise and fallback delivery proof.</p><p>Stop if: Unfunded coverage, unreachable operator or missed class deadline without substitution.</p> 13. Recovery · exit<p>Status: Planned — not run</p><p>Schedule alerts 60/30/14/7 days before expiry; decide continuation at least 30 days before. Rehearse learner/grade export, retention and deletion; verify closure on the approved dates.</p><p>Owner / timing: Service owner + course leads + Libraries / Before start; execute near term end</p><p>Evidence: End-of-term runbook, sample exports, provider confirmation of exit window and deletion/renewal decision.</p><p>Stop if: Relying on grace periods, unconfirmed education extension or archival deposits of private coursework.</p> <p>Download readiness CSV · Return to stages</p> <p>This page records the plan. Selecting or opening a check does not approve it or update readiness.</p>","path":["Before required classroom use"],"tags":[]},{"location":"recommendations.html","level":1,"title":"Recommendations for review","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Draft action framework — priorities and endorsement pending. The working group should refine these using the inventory and faculty/student needs, rather than assume a single platform solves the problem.</p>","path":["Recommendations for review"],"tags":[]},{"location":"recommendations.html#actions-the-college-can-pursue","level":2,"title":"Actions the college can pursue","text":"<ol> <li>Maintain a concise resource inventory. Identify an owner role for each entry and a review process. Publish access routes and limitations that resource owners confirm.</li> <li>Document needs across research and instruction. Use consistent briefs and an agreed university channel; include faculty, students, RSEs and technical staff. Summarize participation and unknowns.</li> <li>Improve discoverability and reuse. Test clear onboarding, reusable environments, shared documentation and relevant training before expanding infrastructure.</li> <li>Scope bounded pilots. Match a small activity to a confirmed route and support window. Measure benefit, access, effort and recovery before making it required.</li> </ol>","path":["Recommendations for review"],"tags":[]},{"location":"recommendations.html#opportunities-for-university-participation","level":2,"title":"Opportunities for university participation","text":"Shared question College contribution What needs university participation Campus compute and hosted AI access Concrete research/classroom scenarios and measured demand Capacity, course access, account structure and service commitments Software, APIs and data agreements Priority tools, datasets, rights and renewal needs Institutional agreements, permitted use and sustainable funding RSE and technical support models Skill/task estimates and existing college commitments Shared staffing, escalation, training and long-term operations Data governance and shared infrastructure Workflow, preservation and collaboration requirements Appropriate review, interoperability and institutional services","path":["Recommendations for review"],"tags":[]},{"location":"recommendations.html#before-prioritizing-an-action","level":2,"title":"Before prioritizing an action","text":"<p>Attach the problem and evidence, users served, owner role, dependencies, effort, funding status, success measure and a review date. Explicitly distinguish an application, exploratory partnership or proposed role from a confirmed commitment.</p> <p>The existing pilot’s $0 cash and Greg’s 10 total hours/week apply to that pilot. They are not a measured college budget or staffing ceiling. Do not assume funding or available staff for these broader recommendations.</p> <p>Recommendation template · Needs assessment · Milestones</p>","path":["Recommendations for review"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html","level":1,"title":"Computing resources report","text":"<p>For the College of Information Science Computing Resources Working Group. This report connects available services, changes in access and a practical response. The resource inventory contains service-specific access, cost and contact details.</p> <p>Review basis: dated provider and campus documentation, mostly checked October 8–19, 2026, plus the explicitly attributed ORAI update. This is a working assessment, not a completed college-wide needs study.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#key-findings","level":2,"title":"Key findings","text":"<ul> <li>Resources exist, but access and support differ. College and campus services, national allocations and bounded free platforms serve different work.</li> <li>Continuity is the gap illustrated by the provider history. Closed services, narrower eligibility, shorter windows and tighter limits can break a course or research workflow.</li> <li>The opportunity is a supported route, not only more hardware. Match activities to existing services, identify the remaining gap, and test shared capacity, expertise and portable environments.</li> </ul>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#available-resources","level":2,"title":"Available resources","text":"<p>The inventory contains 33 entries: eight college, nineteen university and six national/external. It covers hardware, software and models, data, RSE and technical support, training and collaboration. Unknown local equipment, licenses and available support hours are recorded as unknown, rather than assumed absent.</p> <p>Campus starting points include research HPC, software licensing, library data and digital-scholarship services, and research software expertise. Classroom HPC permission needs clarification: the teaching policy names Ocelote, while allocation notices report it retired. Research access does not establish permission to use another cluster for a course.</p> <p>Browse the inventory · Campus AI transition and supplied prices</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#external-routes-and-their-limits","level":3,"title":"External routes and their limits","text":"<p>Open a resource group for its role, status and evidence. These are dated comparisons; availability does not establish account eligibility or reserved capacity.</p> CPU notebooks and development · Available with limits<p>GitHub Codespaces · Binder</p><p>Code, data cleaning, small analyses and reproducible notebook exercises.</p><p>Boundary: Codespaces has monthly personal-account compute/storage limits; Binder uses shared, temporary sessions. Neither reserves a class.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>Codespaces · free personal-account quotas · Binder · free ephemeral teaching notebooks</p> Free GPU experiments · Available with limits<p>Kaggle · free Colab · Paperspace · ZeroGPU</p><p>Small DS/MLIS GPU benchmarks or short model demonstrations, matched to each platform.</p><p>Boundary: Quotas, queues and hardware vary. Paperspace free notebooks are public; ZeroGPU is for bounded demos. Provider runs remain pending.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>Kaggle · free accelerator notebooks · Google Colab Pro for Education · offer closed · Paperspace free machines: visibility and inactivity · Hugging Face ZeroGPU · short shared GPU demos</p> Assignments and evaluation · Available; adoption untested<p>Classroom 50 · Codabench</p><p>Assignment distribution, submissions, scoring and benchmark leaderboards.</p><p>Boundary: Classroom 50 is externally maintained and adds no Codespaces hours. Codabench GPU/code workers need separately supplied compute.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>Classroom 50: project and license · Classroom 50: prerequisites and education eligibility · Codabench · free benchmark hosting and bounded CPU scoring</p> Commercial compute credits · Conditional / promotional<p>Modal · Lightning AI</p><p>Controlled instructor experiments within confirmed credits and billing arrangements.</p><p>Boundary: Modal requires a payment method and limits Starter to three seats. Lightning GPU credits vary. Neither is an approved zero-cash class default.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>Modal Starter pricing and Shared Endpoints boundary · Modal billing: payment method required · Lightning AI · billing and storage scope</p> The proposed classroom baseline · Request / award pending<p>ACCESS CPU + GPU · NAIRR Classroom</p><p>A large measured ACCESS request; additional eligible instructor-led AI pilots through NAIRR.</p><p>Boundary: No award or class capacity is secured. CloudBank is conditional through ACCESS; campus AWS is only for CPU component testing.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>ACCESS · eligibility, educational periods, review, renewal · NAIRR Pilot Classroom · instructor request · CloudBank Classroom · general ACCESS teaching route</p> Other federally supported routes · Eligibility and fit to confirm<p>OSPool / OSG · NRP / Nautilus · MESA / CyVerse</p><p>High-throughput jobs, shared notebooks/GPUs and domain data services, depending on the platform.</p><p>Boundary: Shared scheduling, onboarding and operating duties differ. MESA funding does not establish PATh eligibility or a new GPU entitlement.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>OSPool · opportunistic high-throughput capacity · NRP · classroom products · MESA · published data/AI tools and portal · PATh · affiliated-program eligibility</p> Repositories and scholarly outputs · Surviving routes; distinct roles<p>GitHub Free · GitLab Education · Campus Repository · Zenodo</p><p>Versioned course code, eligible education collaboration, or rights-cleared scholarly deposits.</p><p>Boundary: GitHub retains unlimited repository counts; features/storage remain bounded. Education eligibility and public-deposit rules apply. A live Git repo is not independent preservation.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>GitHub · free repositories still available · GitLab for Education · viable institutional bridge · UArizona · Campus Repository remains free · Zenodo · current storage quota</p> Hosting and small databases · Available with limits<p>GitHub / Cloudflare Pages · D1 · Neon · Supabase</p><p>Static course sites, digital exhibits and bounded database/web assignments.</p><p>Boundary: Static hosting does not supply a general backend. Database quotas, idle behavior, recovery windows and exports need review; working storage is not an archive.</p><p>Detailed comparison →</p><p>GitHub Pages · size and bandwidth limits · Cloudflare Pages · free static hosting limits · Cloudflare D1 · free database limits · Neon · current free Postgres plan · Supabase · current free backend plan</p> <p>Commercial offers and free credits require particular care with payment methods, account scope and paid conversion. Full commercial comparison.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#the-growing-gap","level":2,"title":"The growing gap","text":"What we have previously relied on can no longer be guaranteed <p>The selected history shows losses across compute, classroom workflows, repositories, hosting and databases. It is not a census or a measured decline in total free compute, nor does it establish the size of the college’s unmet demand. GitHub Free repositories and base free Colab remain available.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#documented-changes-by-year","level":3,"title":"Documented changes by year","text":"2022 · Hosting and collaboration limits<ul><li>Nov 28 · Heroku free hosting and databases end<p>Free Dynos / Postgres / Redis → Free plans withdrawn</p><p>Heroku · free compute and databases withdrawn</p></li><li>Dec 28 · new private groups · GitLab constrains private Free groups<p>Private class collaboration → Five-user namespace limit; education exceptions</p><p>GitLab · five-user private-group limit rollout · GitLab · current free user and group limits</p></li></ul> 2023 · Classroom onboarding closes<ul><li>Nov 15 · Replit stops new education teams<p>Create a new classroom team → New Teams for Education blocked</p><p>Replit · Teams for Education decommissioned</p></li></ul> 2024 · Shorter database life and service withdrawals<ul><li>May 20 · new free databases · Render shortens free Postgres lifetime<p>90 days → 30 days</p><p>Render · free Postgres shortened</p></li><li>June 17 · new customers · Google source hosting closes onboarding<p>New Cloud Source Repositories customers → New access closed; existing customers retained</p><p>Google Cloud Source Repositories · new access withdrawn</p></li><li>Aug 1 · Replit removes Teams for Education<p>Existing education product → Education product withdrawn</p><p>Replit · Teams for Education decommissioned</p></li></ul> 2025 · Storage limits, hosting losses and shorter free access<ul><li>May 29 · revised enforcement · Bitbucket limits Free workspace storage<p>Free workspace Git storage → 1 GB aggregate; over-limit read-only</p><p>Bitbucket · free workspace storage and feature reduction · Bitbucket · aggregate Git storage versus LFS</p></li><li>July 8 · Glitch ends project hosting<p>Hosted student apps → Hosting withdrawn</p><p>Glitch · project hosting ended</p></li><li>July 15 · new general Free Tier · AWS changes the free-plan window<p>Legacy 12-month limited benefits → Credit-based free plan: at most six months</p><p>AWS · twelve-month and six-month Free Tier comparison · AWS · credit and free-plan expiration rules</p></li></ul> 2026 · GitHub Classroom ends<ul><li>Aug 28 · GitHub Classroom is decommissioned<p>Classroom website, APIs and automation → Classroom service ended; Git repos remain</p><p>GitHub Classroom · full shutdown</p></li></ul> Change date unknown · observed Oct 8, rechecked Oct 19: Colab Pro for Education: new sign-ups closed. The free one-year education offer no longer accepts new sign-ups. Existing verified subscriptions can continue to their one-year mark. Base free Colab remains; this is not deletion of education accounts. Google Colab Pro for Education · offer closed Upcoming · not yet effective on Oct 19, 2026 · OSF Projects: announced future restrictions<p>New project/component creation is scheduled to stop November 16, 2026; projects are scheduled to become read-only after February 19, 2027. OSF Registries and community preprints are distinct; existing public projects remain accessible.</p><p>OSF · announced Projects withdrawal</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#what-the-college-still-needs-to-establish","level":3,"title":"What the college still needs to establish","text":"<p>Which activities are blocked; who is affected; whether the barrier is capacity, eligibility, awareness, affordability or support; and what existing services can realistically absorb. The resource tool prepares an individual brief. An agreed collection process and participation record are needed before making college-wide claims.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#the-opportunity","level":2,"title":"The opportunity","text":"Avenue Practical next step Commitment to confirm Better use of existing services Map one research and one teaching workflow to a documented route Eligibility, headroom, onboarding and support Shared expertise and portable environments Scope reusable software/data workflows and support tasks Named capacity, task hours, ownership and recurring effort Allocation-backed CPU and GPU Benchmark representative work and justify an ACCESS request Award, provider fit, operations, accounts and classroom use Instructor pilots and partnerships Explore eligible NAIRR Classroom and compatible campus/federal services Scope, access, reporting, support and continuity <p>Instructional-computing case study: the proposed DS/MLIS pilot uses ACCESS for CPU and GPU. Campus AWS is only for component testing; free GPU notebooks support preliminary benchmarks, followed by target-resource replay. CloudBank remains conditional. The pilot’s $0 cash and Greg’s ten total hours/week are its own constraints, not college-wide budget or staffing findings. TA/course-lead effort is separate; paid needs require confirmed eligible grant funding.</p> <p>No award, capacity reservation, provider benchmark or classroom deployment is established here. All 13 pilot readiness checks remain planned. Pilot details and request draft.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#key-considerations","level":2,"title":"Key considerations","text":"Consideration Evidence to seek Activity and service fit Interactive/batch needs, software, memory, concurrency and deadlines Access and reliability Eligible users, whole-term access, queues, quotas and fallback People and cost Support tasks/hours, maintainer, backup, recurring obligations and funding status Data and software rights Permitted data, licenses, retention, exports and recovery Ownership and continuity Institutional control, provider dependencies and an exit path Evaluation and equity Useful outcomes, accessibility, participation and missing perspectives <p>The working-group primers turn these into discussion questions. The approaches and recommendations remain candidates for group review.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#working-group-next-steps","level":2,"title":"Working-group next steps","text":"<p>By the end of Fall 2026, aim for preliminary drafts of the inventory, needs assessment, portfolio of approaches and recommendations. Refine and circulate within the college early in Spring 2027. Identify owner roles, a university channel for collecting needs, resource-owner checks and participation across faculty, students and technical staff.</p> <p>Prioritize practical college actions while separating capacity, agreements and shared staffing questions that need university participation. Working materials.</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"report.html#evidence-and-limitations","level":2,"title":"Evidence and limitations","text":"<p>The historical ledger retains 181 source and verification records, including unavailable sources and their limitations. Campus sources and review dates are attached to inventory entries. Dates of observation, policy effect and recheck are distinct; a page response does not test a signed-in account or available capacity.</p> <p>The Arizona AI Assistant transition, planned $200/month Bedrock credit and supplied model prices come from Wolfgang Jetner at ORAI, relayed by Greg Chism. Recipient scope, renewal, overage rules, migration timing and campus API access still need confirmation. The screenshot was received October 19; its capture/effective date is unknown.</p> <p>No college-wide survey totals, confirmed spare staffing, institutional endorsement or approved funding are inferred. The request tool generates a local brief; optional Luna drafting requires consent for public/synthetic information and does not submit a request to the working group.</p> <p>Source evidence · Chronological research archive · Original audit text · Grouped supplements</p>","path":["Report"],"tags":[]},{"location":"repository-plan.html","level":1,"title":"Repositories, hosting &amp; databases","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Store the right thing in the right place. A Git repository, research deposit, object store, database and running web app have different jobs.</p> Need Routes to assess Continuity requirement Code and assignments GitHub; eligible GitLab education routes Institution-controlled organization, mirrored code and exported configuration Research outputs and metadata Campus Repository / ReDATA, Zenodo, Figshare, discipline repositories Rights, deposit policy, limits, persistent identifiers and versioned exports Library collections and exhibits Omeka and institutional repositories Original files, metadata/schema export, rights and a tested replacement host Static sites GitHub Pages / Cloudflare Pages Local build, portable assets, domain control and an alternate host Databases and app services Neon, Supabase, MongoDB Atlas; eligible hosting routes Current quotas, inactivity/expiry, permitted use, export and restore Active data and delivery Approved provider storage, OSN/OSDF where eligible Independent backup; active storage/cache is not archival preservation","path":["Repositories, hosting &amp; databases"],"tags":[]},{"location":"repository-plan.html#what-changed","level":2,"title":"What changed?","text":"<p>GitLab private Free group limits, Bitbucket aggregate storage caps and Google source-hosting onboarding restrictions constrain specific use. GitHub Free still offers unlimited repository counts. Heroku’s free tiers ended; Glitch hosting ended; several free database products closed or shortened their lifetime. These are distinct policies, not evidence that all repository accounts disappeared.</p> <p>See dated changes and sources →</p>","path":["Repositories, hosting &amp; databases"],"tags":[]},{"location":"repository-plan.html#a-practical-repair-plan","level":2,"title":"A practical repair plan","text":"<ol> <li>Inventory code, files, metadata, app runtimes, databases and ownership separately.</li> <li>Choose a bounded route with an approved fallback; do not assume allocated compute is durable managed hosting.</li> <li>Keep portable environments, schemas, database dumps and rights-cleared file exports.</li> <li>Test restore on a second environment. Proposed targets: a 24-hour recovery point and one-business-day recovery; neither is achieved yet.</li> <li>Export student work, close permissions and use appropriate library archival routes for lasting outputs.</li> </ol> <p>Full findings · All source evidence</p>","path":["Repositories, hosting &amp; databases"],"tags":[]},{"location":"resource-library.html","level":1,"title":"Detailed platform directory","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>For college and university routes, start with the working-group inventory. This detailed directory supports comparison and the compute case study. Start with the work you need to do. Availability does not establish eligibility, capacity or classroom readiness.</p> Need Start here Main limit Small CPU notebooks and code Codespaces / Binder / classroom options Monthly caps, temporary sessions and no class reservation Preliminary GPU benchmarks Kaggle or free Colab Variable hardware, quotas and queues Planned CPU + GPU classroom baseline ACCESS request Award, provider match and classroom checks pending Research batches, notebooks or data services Federal platforms Project eligibility and operating model differ Serverless jobs or hosted inference Modal and alternatives Credits, card requirements and paid conversion Assignments and evaluation Classroom 50 / Codabench Workflow tools do not supply an unrestricted compute allocation Repositories, apps and databases Continuity options Preservation, always-on hosting and compute are different needs <p>Not sure how much? Scope your needs →</p>","path":["Detailed platform directory"],"tags":[]},{"location":"resource-library.html#how-to-choose","level":2,"title":"How to choose","text":"<ol> <li>Define the smallest useful workload, software and outputs.</li> <li>Match CPU, memory, GPU/VRAM, storage and interactive or batch use.</li> <li>Check eligibility, permitted data, payment requirements and the whole-term access window.</li> <li>Measure, test recovery and name support before making it required.</li> </ol> <p>Evidence: the directory reflects dated primary-source reviews, mostly October 8–19, 2026. Check the linked sources before adoption. No signed-in entitlement or provider capacity was tested.</p>","path":["Detailed platform directory"],"tags":[]},{"location":"serverless-compute.html","level":1,"title":"Cloud credits &amp; GPUs","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Modal is a conditional instructor experiment, not the classroom baseline. Its documented $30/workspace/month credit requires a payment method and the Starter workspace has three seats. CPU, GPU and memory share the allowance. No card, credit redemption, grant or workload has been tested here.</p> <p>With $0 cash, do not depend on paid conversion. Use Kaggle/free Colab for preliminary GPU benchmarks, then replay on awarded ACCESS CPU + GPU resources. Confirm eligible grant funding before any paid use.</p> <p>Provider documentation reviewed October 16, 2026. Pricing and credits can change; follow the dated sources.</p> Search resourcesResource typeAll typesBounded free machinesConditional recurring creditLimited model trialPaid / historical free claimPaid API on Free accountsPaid comparisonPaid usage / possible trialPromotional / account-dependentUnconfirmed free lead<p>9 resources</p>Conditional recurring creditModal<p>DS: a small pinned training/inference run. MLIS: public-corpus OCR, embeddings or retrieval experiments.</p><p>Access: $30/workspace/month; three Starter seats</p>Limits and sources<p>Payment method required; GPU, CPU and memory share credits. Set explicit usage and net-spend limits. Shared Endpoints tokens cost extra. Academic grant is an application, not funding in hand.</p><p>Modal Starter pricing and Shared Endpoints boundary · Modal billing: payment method required · Modal budgets and out-of-pocket spend limits · Modal academic credit grants</p>Paid / historical free claimBeam<p>DS/MLIS component hosting if funded; open-source runtime may warrant later engineering assessment.</p><p>Access: $20/month Developer includes $20 credits; one seat</p>Limits and sources<p>June free-$30 comparison is stale against current direct plan table. Prepaid deployments need credits; warm/idle settings can bill. BYOC is not free compute.</p><p>Beam current plan table · Beam runtime and prepaid credit billing · Beam earlier free Developer comparison (historical)</p>Paid usage / possible trialCerebrium<p>DS model serving; MLIS public-corpus embeddings or transcription.</p><p>Access: Free Hobby subscription; no recurring compute credit verified</p>Limits and sources<p>Free plan fee and included storage do not make execution free. Trials are discretionary/time-limited and may transition to paid billing.</p><p>Cerebrium Hobby subscription versus metered compute · Cerebrium trial terms</p>Bounded free machinesPaperspace Gradient<p>Small DS training and public/synthetic MLIS corpus notebooks.</p><p>Access: Free CPU / M4000 GPU subject to availability; 5 GB</p>Limits and sources<p>Six-hour free-session maximum, one-hour idle stop, one free notebook at once; free notebooks are public. Check old GPU/runtime fit and actual signup/payment requirements.</p><p>DigitalOcean Paperspace: free machine and storage eligibility · Paperspace notebook free-session duration · Paperspace free machines: visibility and inactivity</p>Promotional / account-dependentLightning AI<p>DS/MLIS notebook preparation and small tests within actual credit balance.</p><p>Access: One free CPU Studio; GPU credit amount and expiry vary</p>Limits and sources<p>Four-hour CPU restart boundary; first 10 GB storage free. Do not promise a fixed monthly GPU grant or count paid academic discounts as free compute.</p><p>Lightning AI · billing and storage scope · Lightning AI student documentation and free CPU workflow</p>Unconfirmed free leadSaturn Cloud<p>Potential DS and MLIS notebook alternative, pending an authorized account check.</p><p>Access: Landing page advertises 150 compute hours/month, no card</p>Limits and sources<p>150 compute hours is not verified as 150 GPU-hours. Current pricing emphasizes paid hosted resources; reconcile device allowance, storage, inactivity and new-account access.</p><p>Saturn Cloud free-compute landing claim · Saturn Cloud current hosted pricing</p>Paid comparisonRunpod<p>Funded DS/MLIS jobs with explicit checkpoint and external export.</p><p>Access: Prepaid compute; no recurring free allocation verified</p>Limits and sources<p>Pod launch needs credit; stopped storage still bills and can be deleted when funds run out. Not a zero-cash fallback or preservation repository.</p><p>Runpod credit balance and storage lifecycle</p>Limited model trialReplicate<p>DS model-behavior comparison; MLIS controlled metadata/transcription demonstrations.</p><p>Access: Select-model demonstrations, then billing</p>Limits and sources<p>No stable free monthly grant verified. Private deployments can bill setup and idle time. A model API does not replace arbitrary CUDA training.</p><p>Replicate limited free models and billing scope</p>Paid API on Free accountsHugging Face Inference Providers<p>DS inference evaluation and MLIS embeddings/metadata tasks when funded.</p><p>Access: No included monthly API credits for Free users</p>Limits and sources<p>Separate from ZeroGPU/Spaces. Current API pricing overrides older free-credit snippets; no historical change date established.</p><p>Hugging Face Inference Providers: current free-account credits</p>&lt;p&gt;Search needs JavaScript; all resources are shown.&lt;/p&gt;","path":["Cloud credits &amp; GPUs"],"tags":[]},{"location":"supplemental.html","level":1,"title":"Supplement","text":"<p>Detailed references and working materials, grouped by purpose. Start with the overview, resource inventory or report for the main assessment.</p>","path":["Supplement"],"tags":[]},{"location":"supplemental.html#working-group-materials","level":2,"title":"Working-group materials","text":"Material Use Four deliverables Inventory, needs, approaches and recommendations Needs assessment Evaluate barriers and participation without inventing demand Approaches · Recommendations Compare options and separate college actions from university dependencies Milestones · Working session Semester targets and a suggested discussion format Working packet Download the combined working materials Needs register · Recommendation register Blank templates for an agreed university collection process","path":["Supplement"],"tags":[]},{"location":"supplemental.html#platform-references","level":2,"title":"Platform references","text":"Reference Focus Detailed platform directory Match a type of work to comparison pages Federal platforms ACCESS, OSPool, MESA/CyVerse and related services Campus AI transition ORAI update, planned credit and supplied model prices Cloud credits and GPUs Modal and alternatives, including billing boundaries Classroom platforms · Classroom 50 Notebooks, assignment workflows and evaluation tools Repositories, hosting and databases Ownership, storage, exports and preservation Earlier degree comparison Activity-specific examples; not a college-wide needs assessment","path":["Supplement"],"tags":[]},{"location":"supplemental.html#instructional-computing-pilot","level":2,"title":"Instructional-computing pilot","text":"<p>Supporting proposal, not the working group’s selected strategy. ACCESS is the intended CPU + GPU route. Campus AWS is for component testing; bounded free GPUs support preliminary benchmarks. The pilot has $0 cash and Greg’s ten total hours/week; TA and course-lead effort is separate. Allocations, funding and classroom readiness remain unconfirmed.</p> Material Use DS/MLIS pilot · Pilot stages Course concepts and staged decisions Route assessment · ACCESS request Service fit and an unsubmitted request draft Benchmarks · Download runnable tests CPU components and GPU notebooks Optional compute estimator Planning quantities, peak demand and storage Hours and grant dependencies · Classroom checks Support envelope and 13 planned readiness checks","path":["Supplement"],"tags":[]},{"location":"supplemental.html#evidence-and-presentation","level":2,"title":"Evidence and presentation","text":"<ul> <li>Source evidence: 181 historical compute/provider records, with dates and claim boundaries. Campus sources are attached to inventory entries.</li> <li>Chronological research archive · Original audit text: the complete historical research and planning record.</li> <li>Evidence JSON · Inventory CSV · Working-group JSON: downloadable records.</li> <li>Five-slide talk: the original presentation and interactive timeline.</li> <li>Request-tool drafting notes: optional Luna limits, campus handoff and historical model trials.</li> <li>Publishing assessment: authoring-platform notes and design references.</li> </ul>","path":["Supplement"],"tags":[]},{"location":"timeline.html","level":1,"title":"Working-group milestones","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>The working group aims to draft all four deliverables by the end of the Fall 2026 semester, then refine and circulate them within the college early in Spring 2027. These are planning windows, not scheduled meetings or award dates.</p> 01October–November 2026Build the baseline<p>Confirm scope and resource owners; review campus routes; gather research, teaching and student perspectives.</p><p>Output: Versioned inventory, agreed input process and a list of unknowns.</p> 02November–end of Fall 2026 semesterDraft all four deliverables<p>Validate significant barriers; compare approaches; separate college actions from university dependencies.</p><p>Output: Preliminary inventory, needs assessment, portfolio and recommendations.</p> 03Early Spring 2027Refine and circulate within the college<p>Review drafts with faculty, students and resource owners; resolve corrections and agree priorities.</p><p>Output: Revised intra-college drafts with evidence, owners and unresolved questions.</p> 04As university discussions developCarry college priorities forward<p>Prepare the shared-service, policy, staffing and infrastructure questions that require university participation.</p><p>Output: College briefing for anticipated university-wide planning; timing not yet set.</p> <p>Review deliverables · Working-session guide</p>","path":["Working-group milestones"],"tags":[]},{"location":"timeline.html#supporting-compute-pilot-sequence","level":2,"title":"Supporting compute-pilot sequence","text":"<p>The separate CPU/GPU pilot stages depend on measurements, resource approval and classroom checks. Those gates do not define the working group’s semester deadlines.</p>","path":["Working-group milestones"],"tags":[]},{"location":"working-session.html","level":1,"title":"Run a working session","text":"<p>Supplemental reference · Browse supplements · Current report</p> <p>Use a short, repeatable review. This is a suggested meeting format, not a scheduled event or an assigned set of roles.</p>","path":["Run a working session"],"tags":[]},{"location":"working-session.html#before-the-session","level":2,"title":"Before the session","text":"<p>Choose a deliverable and review its current draft. Bring a public or appropriately shareable resource correction or needs brief. Use the group’s agreed university channel for internal material; the public website does not register briefs with the working group.</p>","path":["Run a working session"],"tags":[]},{"location":"working-session.html#suggested-45-minute-agenda","level":2,"title":"Suggested 45-minute agenda","text":"Minutes Activity Record 0–5 Confirm the decision or question Scope and intended outcome 5–15 Review resources and access Verified facts, owners and unknowns 15–25 Discuss needs and barriers Research/teaching differences and missing perspectives 25–35 Compare promising approaches Evidence, prerequisites and a useful small test 35–45 Agree next steps Action, owner role, dependencies and review point","path":["Run a working session"],"tags":[]},{"location":"working-session.html#after-the-session","level":2,"title":"After the session","text":"<p>Update the draft’s date and status. Separate a decision from an open question or suggested action. Record who needs to confirm access, costs, support or permissions; do not list an unconfirmed resource as ready.</p>","path":["Run a working session"],"tags":[]},{"location":"working-session.html#questions-for-the-group-to-settle-first","level":2,"title":"Questions for the group to settle first","text":"<ul> <li>Who maintains each deliverable and confirms factual corrections?</li> <li>Which approved university channel will receive needs briefs and internal notes?</li> <li>How will faculty, students and technical staff contribute, and how will missing perspectives be recorded?</li> <li>Who will review the preliminary drafts before early Spring 2027 circulation?</li> </ul> <p>Four deliverables · Working packet</p>","path":["Run a working session"],"tags":[]}]};